[2020-05-14T23:16:51Z] user_00004: Current `CHIPCryptoTestDriver.c`
```
#include "TestCryptoLayer.h"

#include <nlunit-test.h>

int main(void)
{
    // Generate machine-readable, comma-separated value (CSV) output.
    nlTestSetOutputStyle(OUTPUT_CSV);

    return (TestCHIPCrypto());
}
```

Proposed
```
#include <support/TestUtils.h>

int main(void)
{
    // Generate machine-readable, comma-separated value (CSV) output.
    nlTestSetOutputStyle(OUTPUT_CSV);

    return RunRegisteredUnitTests();
}
```

The `CHIPCryptoTestDriver.c` already calls into `TestUtils` to register its entry point. This will be needed in all other test suites.
```
void __attribute__((constructor)) my_init(void)
{
    printf("Deploying CHIP Crypto PAL tests\n");
    if (CHIP_NO_ERROR != RegisterUnitTests(&TestCHIPCryptoPAL))
    {
        printf("Failed in deploying CHIP Crypto PAL tests\n");
    }
}
```

But, the above code will be needed regardless, as that's how QEMU test app picks up the entry point.

---

[2020-05-15T00:05:57Z] user_00008: Seems like a reasonable pattern. A few modifications I'd suggest:

1. Qualify the constructor with `static` since there's no need to make it a globally-scoped free function.
2. Come up with a naming pattern somewhat more localized and descriptive than `my_init` for the constructor. Using the example above, perhaps `TestCHIPCryptoDriverInit` or `TestCHIPCryptoConstruct` or `TestCHIPCryptoCtor`.
3. Eliminate the printf's in the constructor.
4. In the failure case, use either `VerifyOrDie` or `nlABORT` on successful status from RegisterUnitTests (fail fast).

