body:
 #### Problem
We have the following function on `PacketBuffer`:
```
    uint16_t DataLength(void) const;
    void SetDataLength(uint16_t aNewLen, PacketBuffer * aChainHead = NULL);
    uint16_t AvailableDataLength(void) const;
    static PacketBuffer * NewWithAvailableSize(size_t aAvailableSize);
    static PacketBuffer * NewWithAvailableSize(uint16_t aReservedSize, size_t aAvailableSize);
```
so it generally seems like data length is `uint16_t` but available size bits are `size_t`, except `AvailableDataLength` also returns `uint16_t`.  Which is it?  Is `PacketBuffer` expected to support sizes that don't fit into `uint16_t`?

 #### Proposed Solution
Decide what sizes we actually support, change types as needed, and maybe document clearly.

@user_00004 @user_00003 @user_00005 @user_00008

 #### Problem
Consider this code:
```
void foo(uint16_t arg) {
}
uint32_t bar();
void baz() {
    foo(bar());
}
```

This code, when placed inside CHIP code or one of our sample apps, compiles just fine (failing to link because `bar` is not in fact defined).  It might be better if it did not, because the call to `foo` is dropping the high 2 bytes of the return value of `bar`.  This is particularly relevant because `PacketBuffer` takes its size as `uint16_t` in some cases (see <https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/issues/1633>) so we could run into problems where different parts of the system disagree about the amount of data in a `PacketBuffer`.

 #### Proposed Solution
Add `-Werror=conversion` or equivalent to our build flags.

@user_00017 @user_00003 @user_00005

 Current I am able to make Chip Over Bluez Peripheral connection in Linux device layer. 
When transport/Message layer init, the BLE layer should be hooked there so that it can handle incoming BLE message and negotiate with upper protocol or zcl clusers.

        mBle->mAppState = this;
        mBle->OnChipBleConnectReceived = HandleIncomingBleConnection;
        WeaveLogProgress(MessageLayer, "Accepting WoBLE connections");

 #### Problem
Looking at the code looks like `ChipDeviceController::sendMessage` defaults to sending over the unsecure channel if there is one

```
CHIP_ERROR ChipDeviceController::SendMessage(void * appReqState, PacketBuffer * buffer)
{
    CHIP_ERROR err = CHIP_NO_ERROR;

    VerifyOrExit(mRemoteDeviceId.HasValue(), err = CHIP_ERROR_INCORRECT_STATE);

    mAppReqState = appReqState;

    if (mUnsecuredTransport != NULL)
    {
        VerifyOrExit(IsConnected(), err = CHIP_ERROR_INCORRECT_STATE);
        // Unsecured transport does not use a MessageHeader, but the Transport::Base API expects one, so
        // let build an empty one for now.
        MessageHeader header;
        Transport::PeerAddress peerAddress = Transport::PeerAddress::BLE();
        err                                = mUnsecuredTransport->SendMessage(header, peerAddress, buffer);
    }
    else
    {
        VerifyOrExit(IsSecurelyConnected(), err = CHIP_ERROR_INCORRECT_STATE);
        err = mSessionManager->SendMessage(mRemoteDeviceId.Value(), buffer);
    }
```

Pankaj mentioned this code is temporary and will be removed once some rendezvous pieces land. 
Filing this issue to make sure this is tracked explicitly. 

 #### Proposed Solution
Only use secure transport post pairing.

@user_00014 @user_00006

This function only works if you already have an entropy source.

```
CHIP_ERROR add_entropy_source(entropy_source fn_source, void * p_source, size_t threshold)
{
    CHIP_ERROR error              = CHIP_NO_ERROR;
    int result                    = 0;
    EntropyContext * entropy_ctxt = NULL;

    VerifyOrExit(fn_source != NULL, error = CHIP_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT);

    entropy_ctxt = get_entropy_context();
    VerifyOrExit(entropy_ctxt != NULL, error = CHIP_ERROR_INTERNAL);

    result = mbedtls_entropy_add_source(&entropy_ctxt->entropy, fn_source, p_source, threshold, MBEDTLS_ENTROPY_SOURCE_STRONG);
    VerifyOrExit(result == 0, error = CHIP_ERROR_INTERNAL);
exit:
    return error;
}
```

`get_entropy_context` fails without an initial source because it calls `mbedtls_ctr_drbg_seed`.

 #### Problem
The code looks like this:
```
        const uint16_t valLen     = bleEvent->evt.gatts_evt.params.write.len;
        const uint16_t minBufSize = CHIP_SYSTEM_PACKETBUFFER_HEADER_SIZE + valLen;
```
There is no check that the result of the addition doesn't overflow `uint16_t`.

 #### Proposed Solution
Add a check.  I'm not sure whether it should emit `kCHIPoBLEOutOfBuffersEvent` or do something else....

@user_00004 do you know who generally deals with this code?

 #### Problem
In `src/platform/EFR32/freertos_bluetooth.c` there's a function called `vRaiseEventFlagBasedOnContext` that has this code:
```
    EventBits_t eventBits;
    if (xPortIsInsideInterrupt())
    {
        eventBits = xEventGroupSetBitsFromISR(xEventGroup, uxBitsToWaitFor, &higherPrioTaskWoken);
```
But according to https://www.freertos.org/xEventGroupSetBitsFromISR.html (and the compiler, when compiling with `-Wconversion`!) `xEventGroupSetBitsFromISR` returns `BaseType_t` (aka `long`), not `EventsBits_t` (aka `unsigned long`).  The documentation moreover says that only one of two values is returned `pdPASS` or `pdFAIL`.  This does not seem like a useful thing to assign to an `EventBits_t`.

 #### Proposed Solution
If this function really wants to return the new value of the event group, as it seems to, maybe we should call `xEventGroupGetBitsFromISR` to do that?  

That said, while I am here...  The documentation says to call `portYIELD_FROM_ISR` or `portEND_SWITCHING_ISR` if `pdPASS` is returned, and we're not doing that either, either in this function or at its callsites.  So I have no idea whether any of this ever works as intended.

@user_00002 @user_00014 @user_00006 though I don't know that anyone really knows much about this code... Where did it come from originally?

