body:
 #### Problem
Using latest Android CHIPTool from master branch to scan the QRCode on M5Stack, CHIPTool APP will crash and exit.

The 'mBleLayer' in DeviceController is not initialized. 

In DeviceController::Init function,

#if CONFIG_NETWORK_LAYER_BLE
#if CONFIG_DEVICE_LAYER                         ------ it is defined to 0, the code do not enter this process
    if (params.bleLayer == nullptr)
    {
        params.bleLayer = DeviceLayer::ConnectivityMgr().GetBleLayer();
    }
#endif // CONFIG_DEVICE_LAYER
    mBleLayer = params.bleLayer;               ------- params.bleLayer is nullptr makes the mBleLayer be nullptr
    VerifyOrExit(mBleLayer != nullptr, err = CHIP_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT);
#endif

mAppData is used for ReclaimEventCtx, which has 
struct ReclaimEventCtx
{
    CircularEventBuffer * mpEventBuffer = nullptr;
    size_t mSpaceNeededForMovedEvent    = 0;
};

when debug buffer is full, we try to evict event to next buffer, we need to get to know how much space for evicted event in debug buffer 

EvictHead function(https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/blob/master/src/lib/core/CHIPCircularTLVBuffer.cpp#L117) would check and call this mProcessEvictedElement function, this function could be AlwaysFail or EvictEvent, if that is EvictEvent, it will check if the tail's event can be moved to higher priority, if not, dropped, if yes, note how much space it needs.

I would use delegate to get rid void pattern in follow-up PR, thanks

_Originally posted by @user_00030 in https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/6366#discussion_r624942829_

 #### Problem

Currently, SystemLayer provides an abstraction layer to the System::Timer class. We found this APIs design is pretty confusing and not ergonomic
 
1.    Error NewTimer(Timer *& aTimerPtr);
The purpose of SystemLayer abstraction is to prevent the upper layer access the low level System::Timer class directly.  But this API allow the upper code get a raw handle of timer object and manipulate this object with  System::Timer class APIs directly.

2.   void StartTimer(uint32_t aMilliseconds, chip::Callback::Callback<> * aCallback);
This API start the platform timer with aCallback, but does not provide the relative API to stop the platform timer.  

3. Error StartTimer(uint32_t aMilliseconds, TimerCompleteFunct aComplete, void * aAppState);
    void CancelTimer(TimerCompleteFunct aOnComplete, void * aAppState);

These APIs start and stop the timer with combination of TimerCompleteFunct and AppState, most of morden timer design use timer handle to implement similar functions.

One of benefits of this design is each user of system timer does not need to maintain the timer object, it can just use the completeFunc and AppState(this pointer) to identify the timer.

 #### Proposed Solution
Revisit the current timer APIs design to make it more intuitive

### Problem

CommandSender::OnMessageReceived first ensures that the incoming apExchangeContext is equal to its own mpExchangeCtx. Then it calls ProcessCommandMessage and deletes its mpExchangeCtx thinking that it's the same one as at the beginning of the function. In my case many times it isn't, it's already the next incoming message, which due to the deletion gets lost. With CRMP this causes retransmission, while with reliable transport the new message gets lost forever.

### Suggested solutions

1 - quick fix: after calling ProcessCommandMessage check again whether mpExchangeCtx changed and don't close the unprocessed context, e.g.

if (apExchangeContext == mpExchangeCtx)
{
  mpExchangeCtx->Close();
  mpExchangeCtx = nullptr;
  Reset();
}

2 - fix all possible external code to make sure this does not happen, ever.

 #### Problem

Double-lock from this commit:

```
commit fa07bbfe1c11cdde9f79b6c5870f2eae3923a5d6
Author: Sagar Dhawan <sagar_dhawan@apple.com>
Date:   Wed Mar 18 12:49:08 2020 -0700

    Bring in EFR32 platform files
```

```
int GetEntropy_EFR32(uint8_t * buf, size_t count)
{
    int res = 0;

    VerifyOrDie(count <= UINT16_MAX);

#if defined(_SILICON_LABS_32B_SERIES_1)
#if CHIP_DEVICE_CONFIG_ENABLE_THREAD
    if (ThreadStackManagerImpl::IsInitialized())
    {
        ThreadStackMgr().LockThreadStack();
    }
#endif // CHIP_DEVICE_CONFIG_ENABLE_THREAD

#if CHIP_DEVICE_CONFIG_ENABLE_THREAD
    if (ThreadStackManagerImpl::IsInitialized())
    {
        ThreadStackMgr().LockThreadStack();
    }
#endif // CHIP_DEVICE_CONFIG_ENABLE_THREAD
```
Likely due to duplication during merge.

 #### Proposed Solution
 
Remove duplicated code.

 #### Problem
```
void CriticalSectionEnter()
{
    int err = pthread_mutex_lock(&eventLocker);
    assert(err == 0);
}
```
and similar for CriticalSectionExit.  On error, it silently presses on in non-debug builds.

 #### Proposed Solution
Make these functions fallible and check for failure appropriately.

 #### Problem
FieldId and ListIndex should not be exclusive in attribute Path.
If targeting an item in a list-typed attribute we would do node id, endpoint id, cluster id, attribute (field) id, list index

if (apAttributePathParamsList[index].mFlags == AttributePathFlags::kFieldIdValid)
                {
                    attributePathBuilder.FieldId(apAttributePathParamsList[index].mFieldId);
                }
                else if (apAttributePathParamsList[index].mFlags == AttributePathFlags::kListIndexValid)
                {
                    attributePathBuilder.ListIndex(apAttributePathParamsList[index].mListIndex);
                }
                else
                {
                    err = CHIP_ERROR_INVALID_ARGUMENT;
                    ExitNow();
                }
 #### Proposed Solution
<suggested fix, suggested enhancement>

