body:
 #### Problem

From CHIPDeviceController.cpp at commit 53e7847294a531aa4d99b38ef5b38850cb1fe868:

```
CHIP_ERROR DeviceCommissioner::OnOperationalCredentialsProvisioningCompletion(Device * device)
{
...
    // Note - This assumes storage is synchronous, the device must be in storage before we can cleanup
    // the rendezvous session and mark pairing success
    PersistDevice(device);
    // Also persist the device list at this time
    // This makes sure that a newly added device is immediately available
    PersistDeviceList();
```

We have no concept of transactions in the `CHIPPersistentStorageDelegate.h`.  All we can assume is that in quality implementations, persistence stores will be ordered and atomic.

In the above CHIPDeviceController snippet, this means that a device may be persisted, but the device list may not.  That would cause the sdk to effectively 'lose' the device forever because we also do not have any sort of iterator or filter facility in the persistence interface; there is no way for `CHIPDeviceController` to arbitrarily find all devices in the persistent data store, so it can only locate devices already known to itself.  In addition to creating inconsistent persisted state, this scenario is also a storage leak.

 #### Proposed Solution

The most straight forward solution now would be to swap the order of `PersistDeviceList` and `PersistDevice`.  If `PersistDeviceList` is called first and succeeds, but `PersistDevice` does not, when `CHIPDeviceController` wakes, it can know that a device is missing in the persistent store and actually do something about it.

Alternatively, we could add iterators or filter/search capabilities to the persistent data interface to allow code like `CHIPDeviceController` to e.g. locate all keys with a given prefix.  That would obviate the need to persist both devices and a device list, and would prevent the possibility of at least this inconsistency in the persistent data store.

