[2021-07-09T07:28:39Z] user_00015: Just found the root cause of this issue. The dbus mainloop in CHIP gives little chance for other threads to acquire the mutex of dbus connection. The issue is gone if adding `sleep(0);` after the following line:
https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/blob/9c01258cbd4342d2ca45f0d5912e3cfc3d4efe25/src/platform/Linux/ThreadStackManagerImpl.cpp#L101

body:
 #### Problem
This arose in discussions in #7325.  We have a lot of code calling memmove like this in and around attribute-size.cpp:

```
void copyListMember(uint8_t * dest, uint8_t * src, bool write, uint16_t * offset, uint16_t length)
{
    if (write)
    {   
        memmove(dest + *offset, src, length);
    }   
    else
    {   
        memmove(dest, src + *offset, length);
    }   

    *offset = static_cast<uint16_t>(*offset + length);
}
```

This implies that callers may be able to support in-place manipulation of src/dest buffers.  That's misleading though because if many separate memmove operations occur on the src/dest buffers (as will usually be the case), then in-place manipulation is actually not safe except for the narrow case where src and dest are the same address and there is no macro reorganization of src as it is moved to the destination buffer.

Consider:

````
void macro_memmove(uint8_t *src, uint8_t *dst)
{
    memmove(dst, src, 2);
    memmove(dst + 2, src + 2, 2);
    // oops; if dst == src + 2, this breaks
}
```

So in fact, we actually cannot support overlapping src/dst buffers like a single memmove call can.  And, we don't use overlapping buffers anyway.  Thus, it makes sense to refactor this code to use memcpy throughout instead of memmove.

This will be slightly more performant and will reduce the likelihood that this code could be misconstrued to support overlapping buffers, when it actually does not.

 #### Proposed Solution

Refactor attribute-size.cpp and related code to call memcpy instead of memmove.

 #### Problem
If I look at `MediaPlaybackManager`, it has things like this:
```
bool emberAfMediaPlaybackClusterMediaFastForwardCallback(chip::app::Command *)
{
    EmberAfMediaPlaybackStatus status = MediaPlaybackManager().proxyMediaPlaybackRequest(MediaPlaybackRequest::FastForward);
    sendResponse("MediaFastForward", ZCL_MEDIA_FAST_FORWARD_RESPONSE_COMMAND_ID, status);
    return true;
}
```
where `sendResponse` encodes a response in the ZCL encoding, which does not match the Matter command/response encoding.  This is obviously broken, in that the sender will not be able to decode the response, but nothing in CI tests this, so no one notices.

 #### Proposed Solution
Encode the response commands properly and add CI for tv-app.

@user_00134

 #### Problem
See https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/7651#discussion_r652328711 for the problem description.  The `shutdown` method there has this general shape:
```
dispatch_async(_chipWorkQueue, ^{
  // Tear down things here
});

StopEventLoopTask();
```

and as @user_00018 points out this can race: if something dispatches to the queue between when our shutdown task runs and when the `StopEventLoopTask` task runs, it would run in a world where stuff is partially torn-down.

 #### Proposed Solution
@user_00018 and I came up with a few options:
1. Do `StopEventLoopTask()` and then do whatever platform teardown needs to happen synchronously after that, not off the work queue.  This _should_ be safe in terms of the CHIP bits; not clear whether it's safe in terms of the member access on the ObjC CHIPDeviceController.  Maybe we would need a task that nulls out the members and stashes them in stack locals, then shut down the queue, then do the teardowns of the now-stack-locals?
2. Have a way to do the work that `StopEventLoopTask` does but without a separate dispatch.  Then we could `dispatch_sync` a block that does that work and then tears things down.
3. Have `StopEventLoopTask` take a callback/block/whatever that it calls when it's done stopping things; we would do the teardown there.

@user_00014 any other bright ideas?

#### Problem
AdminPairingInfo has a large linear blob of storage for one set of opcerts for a given fabric:

```
    struct StorableAdminPairingInfo
    {
        uint16_t mAdmin;    /* This field is serialized in LittleEndian byte order */
        uint64_t mNodeId;   /* This field is serialized in LittleEndian byte order */
        uint64_t mFabricId; /* This field is serialized in LittleEndian byte order */
        uint16_t mVendorId; /* This field is serialized in LittleEndian byte order */

        uint16_t mRootCertLen; /* This field is serialized in LittleEndian byte order */
        uint16_t mOpCertLen;   /* This field is serialized in LittleEndian byte order */

        Crypto::P256SerializedKeypair mOperationalKey;
        uint8_t mRootCert[kMaxChipCertSize];
        // The operationa credentials set can have up to two certs -> ICAC and NOC
        uint8_t mOperationalCert[kMaxChipCertSize * 2];
        char mFabricLabel[kFabricLabelMaxLengthInBytes + 1] = { '\0' };
    };
```

This contains 3x opcerts at kMaxChipCertSize (600).

Furthermore, the `OperationalCredentialSet` expects to keep all of these in-memory, per fabric, in a way that they cannot be used in place.

Both of these together cause a large amount of data to be maintained in RAM on embedded platform, when they are actually very infrequently used (only during case), and only ever used one at a time.

This prevents memory optimizations on memory-starved devices and is an obstacle to fitting multi-fabric support on some low-RAM targets. Already some Silabs parts testing commissioning flow with real opcerts are running out of heap due to fragmentation because of these large blocks.

 #### Proposed Solution

This particular problem can be addressed in several steps:

1. Communicating to the dev team that operational certs are infrequently used, and their lookup from non-volatile storage on-demand is a very small cost compared to the time cost of signature verifications in which they take part, and memory cost in maintaining them in RAM working set permanently.
2. Split kMaxChipCertSize from 600 (which is the max for DER certs for Device Attestation and for DER version of opcerts) to two values:
  - kMaxChipOpCertSize = 400 -> The maximum size of a TLV cert
  - kMaxCHIPDerCertSize = 600 -> The guaranteed max size of a DER cert like for DAC/PAI/PAA, and opcert at commissioner prior to TLV conversion
  - **This is a savings of 33%**
3. Refactor OperationalCredentialSet and code using it, to never expect an "already in-place" version, and rather to look them up from persisted storage every time. **Currently this keeps copies of all elements of the opcert chain in HEAP** (see AdminPairingInfo::SetXXXCert which allocs into heap). Release is done at the end of CASE, but the whole set (3x certs) is needed in memory at a time. If N incoming CASE sessions are in progress, Nx3 certs are maintained.
4. Introduce a `TlvCertificateStore` interface, which can be provided *by platform*  to enable optimization of storage/lookup of operational certs.

All primitives in the spec (and their related implementations) should be able to work with OpCerts one by one. They do not need to be maintained in memory permanently, or contiguously.

The following interface **sketch** would fulfill all requirements of Matter Operational Certs:

- Allow removal of roots by SKID
- Allow lookup of "next in chain" by AKID/SKID
- Allow lookup of NOC by full node reference if needed (PK + fabric id  + node id)
- Add simple implementation of `AddTrustedRootCertificate`/`RemoveTrustedRootCertificate`/`AddOpCert`/`UpdateOpCert`/`RemoveFabric` to spec with no dangling issues and no duplication of certificates
- Allow optimization of large blob storage by platform, since OpCerts are the largest contributor to non-volatile stored state in quantity and size
- Make clear that you can just look-up the certs in a temp buffer.

```
// Store/Retrieve TLV certificates by properties.
// Has all the primitives that may be needed to implement
// Matter operational certs
class TlvCertificateStore
{
 public:
  // Search by SKID --> Good to find known cert / next in chain
  virtual CHIP_ERROR GetBySkid(
    const uint8_t* cert_skid,
    size_t skid_len,
    uint8_t * cert_buffer_out,
    size_t cert_buffer_len) = 0;

  // Search by Public Key --> Used by GetByFullFabricRef
  virtual CHIP_ERROR GetByPublicKey(
    const uint8_t* public_key,
    size_t public_key_len,
    uint8_t * cert_buffer_out,
    size_t cert_buffer_len) = 0;

  // Good to find a NOC, so that chain can be followed
  virtual CHIP_ERROR GetByFullFabricRef(
    const uint8_t* public_key,
    size_t public_key_len,
    uint64_t fabric_id,
    uint64_t node_id,
    uint8_t * cert_buffer_out,
    size_t cert_buffer_len) = 0;

  // Insert any cert
  virtual CHIP_ERROR Insert(uint8_t * cert_buffer, size_t cert_buffer_len) = 0;

  // Remove by public key. Unambiguous.
  virtual CHIP_ERROR RemoveByPublicKey(const uint8_t* public_key, size_t public_key_len) = 0;
  // Remove by content. Unambiguous.
  virtual CHIP_ERROR RemoveByCertContent(const uint8_t* cert_buffer, size_t cert_buffer_len) = 0;
};
```

