Also, I have no idea what `FinalSyncInterval` is.  It's not in the actual message definitions in the IM spec; "8.5.3. Subscribe Response Action" has "MinInterval" and "MaxInterval".  It's in the encoding spec, of course, but all that means is that the encoding spec can't encode what the actual messages are supposed to be in its current state...

@user_00018 @user_01447

_Originally posted by @user_00012 in https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/issues/8308#issuecomment-878637392_

 #### Problem

The spec defines PBKDFParamRequest message as:
```
struct {
  byte randomFromInitiator[32];
  byte initiatorSessionId[2];
  byte passcodeID[2];
  boolean hasPBKDFParameters;
} PBKDFParamRequest
```

Yet the implementation in `PASESession::SendPBKDFParamRequest` only constructs the first field.
 
 #### Proposed Solution
Fix implementation to match spec.

 #### Problem

Nothing in the spec precludes an initiator from establishing multiple concurrent sessions with a peer.  But the SDK is broken for such a use case.  #8481 contributes, but the following also explicitly disallows / breaks this:

```
CHIP_ERROR SecureSessionMgr::NewPairing(const Optional<Transport::PeerAddress> & peerAddr, NodeId peerNodeId,
                                        PairingSession * pairing, SecureSession::SessionRole direction, Transport::AdminId admin)
{
    uint16_t peerKeyId          = pairing->GetPeerKeyId();
    uint16_t localKeyId         = pairing->GetLocalKeyId();
    PeerConnectionState * state = mPeerConnections.FindPeerConnectionState(Optional<NodeId>::Value(peerNodeId), peerKeyId, nullptr);

    // Find any existing connection with the same node and key ID
    if (state && (state->GetAdminId() == Transport::kUndefinedAdminId || state->GetAdminId() == admin))
    {
        mPeerConnections.MarkConnectionExpired(
            state, [this](const Transport::PeerConnectionState & state1) { HandleConnectionExpired(state1); });
    }
```

Callers into the sdk should not be prevented from establishing multiple concurrent sessions with a peer.

 #### Proposed Solution

Remove code from sdk that prevents multiple concurrent sessions with a peer.

 #### Problem
As of (SHA: [b0c96ee](https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/blob/b0c96ee73af4ce1dc6894763f6a78855f8be6e79/src/protocols/secure_channel/CASESession.cpp)),

In the following code snippet, CASESession prematurely considers Pairing complete and Session establish upon successful transmission of SigmaR3 message.

```
CHIP_ERROR CASESession::SendSigmaR3()
{
    ...
    // Call delegate to send the Msg3 to peer

    err = mExchangeCtxt->SendMessage(Protocols::SecureChannel::MsgType::CASE_SigmaR3, std::move(msg_R3));
    SuccessOrExit(err);

    ChipLogDetail(SecureChannel, "Sent SigmaR3 msg");

    err = mCommissioningHash.Finish(messageDigestSpan);
    SuccessOrExit(err);

    mPairingComplete = true;

    // Forget our exchange, as no additional messages are expected from the peer
    mExchangeCtxt = nullptr;

    // Call delegate to indicate pairing completion
    mDelegate->OnSessionEstablished();
   ...
}
```

I believe that CASESession.cpp should wait until it has received the Ack from the other node before considering Pairing complete or that Session is established. While there is a high likelihood that the SigmaR3 message will succeed, but the proper approach here would be to assume that SigmaR3 can potentially fail to be sent successfully in a production environment. 



 #### Proposed Solution
Wait until we have received the ACK for SigmaR3 message before considering session establish or CASE Sussuccesful and handle errors in acknowledge as CASE/Session not established.

 #### Problem

The CHIP Device Callbacks manager seems to be using legacy Zigbee concepts to track callbacks:

```
    struct ResponseCallbackInfo
    {
        chip::NodeId nodeId;
        uint8_t sequenceNumber;

        bool operator==(ResponseCallbackInfo const & other)
        {
            return nodeId == other.nodeId && sequenceNumber == other.sequenceNumber;
        }
    };
```

This was observed while controlling two nodes with different fabric IDs, but the same node ID.

There are a number of problems with this:

* this is broken for multi-fabric, where node ID isn't unique
* this constrains to a single concurrent session with a given peer
* this seems to be using sequence number to order data, when this isn't necessary; the secure channel gives this implicitly
* this is a security vulnerability because responses can be misdirected to internal context; this creates a potential for privilege escalation

 #### Proposed Solution

The simplest thing to do is to key on session ID.  That will by definition always be correct.

 #### Problem
`CommandHandler::ProcessCommandDataElement` does:
```
    err = commandPath.GetEndpointId(&endpointId);
    SuccessOrExit(err);
```
but this will fail for group paths.  What should instead happen is that if there is no endpoint id we check for a group id (which is NOT in the command path; it's in the packet header, iirc), and then loop over all endpoints in the group doing the command on each endpoint.  

 #### Proposed Solution
Fix things so group commands work, like they used to work in the Ember code.

