_Originally posted by @user_00012 in https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/8923#discussion_r687917074_

 #### Problem
* Accessor functions do not set default values for attributes in case the ember read function fails.
> EmberAfStatus GetColorLoopTime(chip::EndpointId endpoint, uint16_t * colorLoopTime)
{
    return emberAfReadServerAttribute(endpoint, ColorControl::Id, Ids::ColorLoopTime, (uint8_t *) colorLoopTime,
                                      sizeof(*colorLoopTime));
}

 #### Proposed Solution
* Set attributes in accessor function to default value in case ember read fails

 #### Problem
`retrieveOptionalInfoString` does:
```
    const uint32_t valLength = reader.GetLength();
    chip::Platform::ScopedMemoryBuffer<char> value;
    value.Alloc(valLength + 1);
```

 #### Proposed Solution
Don't even allocate.  We're about to copy into an `std::string` anyway.  Just grab the pointer to the in-tlv data (which will do length-verification at least to the extent of it not overflowing the tlv data itself) and use the 2-arg form of `std::string::assign`.

 #### Problem
CASESession.cpp has code like this in a few places:
```
    VerifyOrExit(msg_R2_Encrypted.Alloc(tlvReader.GetLength()), err = CHIP_ERROR_NO_MEMORY);
    msg_r2_encrypted_len_with_tag = tlvReader.GetLength();
    VerifyOrExit(msg_r2_encrypted_len_with_tag > kTAGSize, err = CHIP_ERROR_INVALID_TLV_ELEMENT);
    SuccessOrExit(err = tlvReader.GetBytes(msg_R2_Encrypted.Get(), static_cast<uint32_t>(msg_r2_encrypted_len_with_tag)));
```
which will try to allocate a large buffer if there's a malicious/invalid length value in the TLV.

Worse yet, the `GetBytes` API documentation says:
```
     * To determine the required input buffer size, call the GetLength() method before calling GetBytes().
```
which is exactly what the code above does and what we don't want to do.

 #### Proposed Solution
Rewrite more like this:
```
    ByteSpan encryptedBytes;
    SuccessOrExit(err = tlvReader.GetBytes(encryptedBytes));
    // Need to make a copy because we will mutate the bytes.
    msg_r2_encrypted_len_with_tag = encryptedBytes.size();
    VerifyOrExit(msg_r2_encrypted_len_with_tag > kTAGSize, err = CHIP_ERROR_INVALID_TLV_ELEMENT);
    VerifyOrExit(msg_R2_Encrypted.Alloc(msg_r2_encrypted_len_with_tag), err = CHIP_ERROR_NO_MEMORY);
    memcpy(msg_R2_Encrypted.Get(), encryptedBytes.data(), msg_r2_encrypted_len_with_tag);
```
or so.  And audit all the other GetLength() callers in this file for sure, possibly in our tree.  And think hard about whether that 2-arg `GetBytes` signature on the TLV reader should even exist....  Maybe we should just have a GetBytes that gets you a span and a `GetBytes` template that takes an array literal (so you presumably did not allocate it using GetLength())...

@user_00093 in case you end up with time for this before I get to it week after next.

 #### Problem
The `DeviceController` currently instantiates its own singletons like so:
```
CHIP_ERROR DeviceController::Init(ControllerInitParams params)
{
...
    mTransportMgr          = chip::Platform::New<DeviceTransportMgr>();
    mSessionMgr            = chip::Platform::New<SecureSessionMgr>();
    mExchangeMgr           = chip::Platform::New<Messaging::ExchangeManager>();
    mMessageCounterManager = chip::Platform::New<secure_channel::MessageCounterManager>();
```
This can cause some certain problems. For example, an OTA Provider must implement BDX and needs to call `ExchangeManager::RegisterUnsolicitedMessageHandlerForProtocol()` to accept BDX transfer requests, but it would need access to the `ExchangeManager` contained by `DeviceController`.

 #### Proposed Solutions
A) Inject dependencies by adding `ExchangeManager` and others to `ControllerInitParams`
B) Provide an access method for getting a pointer/reference to the `ExchangeManager` instantiated by `DeviceController`

 #### Problem

It seems for array types in commands callback, the encoder and decoder aren't finalized yet. One example you can see is for [ScanNetworks command](https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/blob/b683e6aaaa139624bce72ba797aa797dfa73616c/src/controller/java/zap-generated/ChipClusters.java#L3504):

```
    public interface ScanNetworksResponseCallback {
      void onSuccess(int errorCode, String debugText
          // wifiScanResults: /* TYPE WARNING: array array defaults to */ uint8_t *
          // Conversion from this type to Java is not properly implemented yet
          // threadScanResults: /* TYPE WARNING: array array defaults to */ uint8_t *
          // Conversion from this type to Java is not properly implemented yet
          );

      void onError(Exception error);
    }
```

wifiScanResults is missing in JAVA implementation, similar for iOS.

 #### Proposed Solution
Fix the issue.

 #### Problem
`PrepareListFromTLV()` is sometimes generated in `CHIPClientCallbacks.cpp` but not used, and this causes compiler errors due to `[-Werror=unused-function`]:

```
../../examples/ota-requestor/linux/third_party/connectedhomeip/zzz_generated/ota-requestor-app/zap-generated/CHIPClientCallbacks.cpp:351:22: error: ‘EmberAfStatus PrepareListFromTLV(chip::TLV::TLVReader*, const uint8_t*&, uint16_t&)’ defined but not used [-Werror=unused-function]
  351 | static EmberAfStatus PrepareListFromTLV(TLV::TLVReader * tlvData, const uint8_t *& message, uint16_t & messageLen)
      |                      ^~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~
```

To be more specific: `PrepareListFromTLV()` is always generated for any application with client clusters, but the usage of `PrepareListFromTLV()` is only generated under the following conditions:
```
{{#chip_client_clusters}}
{{#if (chip_server_has_list_attributes)}}
{{#chip_server_cluster_attributes}}
{{#if isList}}
void {{asUpperCamelCase parent.name}}Cluster{{asUpperCamelCase name}}ListAttributeFilter(TLV::TLVReader * tlvData, Callback::Cancelable * onSuccessCallback, Callback::Cancelable * onFailureCallback)
{
    // TODO: Add actual support for array and lists.
    const uint8_t * message = nullptr;
    uint16_t messageLen     = 0;
    EmberAfStatus res = PrepareListFromTLV(tlvData, message, messageLen);
```

So compiling will fail for an application with client clusters, but where the corresponding server cluster does not have list attributes.

 #### Proposed Solution
I proposed an outline for a fix in: https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/8772. This involves writing a new javascript function `chip_any_server_has_list_attributes` to guard generation of `PrepareListFromTLV()`.

 #### Problem

In `AES_CCM_encrypt` in `src/crypto/CHIPCryptoPALOpenSSL.cpp`:

```
    // 16 bytes key for AES-CCM-128
    type = (key_length == 16) ? EVP_aes_128_ccm() : EVP_aes_256_ccm();
```

and in `_isValidKeyLength` in `src/crypto/CHIPCryptoPALmbedTLS.cpp`:

```
static bool _isValidKeyLength(size_t length)
{
    // 16 bytes key for AES-CCM-128, 32 for AES-CCM-256
    if (length == 16 || length == 32)
    {
        return true;
    }
    return false;
}
```

AES-CCM-256 is not in the spec for V1, and it is quite a lot of flash to support. 

 #### Proposed Solution

The support of AES-256 should be removed or made compile-time-optional for V1.0 since it's not part of the spec.

 #### Problem
`ASN1Writer::PutBitString(uint8_t unusedBitCount, chip::TLV::TLVReader & encodedBits)` and `ASN1Writer::PutValue(uint8_t cls, uint32_t tag, bool isConstructed, chip::TLV::TLVReader & val)` have similar code that looks dangerous to me.  Quoting from `PutValue`:
```
    valLen = val.GetLength();

    ReturnErrorOnFailure(EncodeHead(cls, tag, isConstructed, valLen));

    val.GetBytes(mWritePoint, valLen);
    mWritePoint += valLen;

    return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
```

What happens if the TLV is malicious or malformed and `GetLength()` returns a length that is larger than the amount of data actually available in the reader?  We will encode that length, then the `GetBytes` call will fail (possibly after copying some number of bytes smaller than `valLen`), then we will adjust our write point and return success.  But we won't actually have initialized all the bytes we are claiming to be putting into our buffer.

Also, the use of `EncodeHead` here is unsafe and can lead to a buffer overrun with malicious TLV.  The way `EncodeHead` works is that it does:
```
    totalLen = 1 + bytesForLen + (len != kUnknownLength ? len : 0);
    VerifyOrReturnError((mWritePoint + totalLen) <= mBufEnd, ASN1_ERROR_OVERFLOW);
```
Let's say `len` (which is the return value from `GetLength()` and can be assumed to be malicious) is `0xFFFFFFFA` (aka `(uint32_t)-6`.  Then `bytesForLen` will be 5, `totalLen` will end up as 0, the `mWritePoint + totalLen <= mBufEnd` test will presumably succeed.  The TLV presumably does not have that much data in it, of course, but if we have a non-contiguous reader it does not know how much it has, and will keep copying data out in `GetBytes` until it runs out of data.  If it provides more data than `mBufEnd - mWritePoint`, it will overrun the end of the ASN1Writer's buffer and start writing memory we don't own, before erroring out when it discovers it does not actually have `0xFFFFFFFA` bytes of data.

 #### Proposed Solution
At a bare minimum, we need to fail out without adjusting `mWritePoint` if `GetBytes` fails.  But that won't address the EncodeHead problem or the fact that we encode a bogus length into the buffer before erroring out.

Even better might be to require a `ContiguousBufferTLVReader` and use `GetByteView`, which will at least ensure that we have all the data to back up the length we thing we are dealing with.

We could fix the EncodeHead problem by adjusting the checks to avoid overflow.  Something like this:
```
  VerifyOrReturnError(mWritePoint <= mBufEnd, ASN1_ERROR_OVERFLOW);  // Or not, if this is an invariant we maintain anyway
  VerifyOrReturnError(mBufEnd - mWritePoint >= 1 + bytesForLen, ASN1_ERROR_OVERFLOW); // Make sure we don't underflow
  VerifyOrReturnError(mBufEnd - mWritePoint - (1 + bytesForLen) >= (len != kUnknownLength ? len : 0), ASN1_ERROR_OVERFLOW);
```

 #### Problem
    
ExchangeContext::ExchangeContext() -- mDispatch is derived from mDelegate with mDelegate being set via an input argument. When exchanges are destroyed, the ExchangeContext's mDelegate is set to nullptr, but mDispatch is untouched.  This will result in a crash since in all likelihood, when mDelegate is set to nullptr, it can be assumed the derived mDispatch is also destroyed as a result, but not updated.
    
#### Proposed Solution

The following workaround is effective, but the real solution is probably some generally improved lifecycle management in and around the ExchangeContext object.

```
diff --git a/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.cpp b/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.cpp
index b5469d26e..76cbd57ef 100644
--- a/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.cpp
+++ b/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.cpp
@@ -247,11 +247,13 @@ ExchangeContext::ExchangeContext(ExchangeManager * em, uint16_t ExchangeId, Sess
         if (dispatch == nullptr)
         {
             dispatch = &em->mDefaultExchangeDispatch;
+            mDelegateDefaultedToApplicationExchangeDispatch = true;
         }
     }
     else
     {
         dispatch = &em->mDefaultExchangeDispatch;
+        mDelegateDefaultedToApplicationExchangeDispatch = true;
     }
     VerifyOrDie(dispatch != nullptr);
     mDispatch = dispatch->Retain();
@@ -269,6 +271,20 @@ ExchangeContext::ExchangeContext(ExchangeManager * em, uint16_t ExchangeId, Sess
 
 ExchangeContext::~ExchangeContext()
 {
+    if (mDelegate == nullptr && !mDelegateDefaultedToApplicationExchangeDispatch)
+    {
+        // in ExchangeContext::ExchangeContext() -- mDispatch is derived from mDelegate with mDelegate being set
+        // via an input argument. When exchanges are destroyed, the ExchangeContext's mDelegate is set to nullptr,
+        // but mDispatch is untouched, this will result in a crash since in all likelihood, when mDelegate is set
+        // to nullptr, it can be assumed the derived mDispatch is also destroyed as a result, but not updated.
+        ChipLogProgress(ExchangeManager, "~ExchangeContext: %u, mDelegate is null for non Application exchanges. Setting dispatch to null", mExchangeId);
+        mDispatch = nullptr;
+    }
     VerifyOrDie(mExchangeMgr != nullptr && GetReferenceCount() == 0);
     VerifyOrDie(!IsAckPending());
 
@@ -389,6 +405,20 @@ CHIP_ERROR ExchangeContext::HandleMessage(const PacketHeader & packetHeader, con
                                           const Transport::PeerAddress & peerAddress, MessageFlags msgFlags,
                                           PacketBufferHandle && msgBuf)
 {
+    if (mDelegate == nullptr && !mDelegateDefaultedToApplicationExchangeDispatch)
+    {
+        // in ExchangeContext::ExchangeContext() -- mDispatch is derived from mDelegate with mDelegate being set
+        // via an input argument. When exchanges are destroyed, the ExchangeContext's mDelegate is set to nullptr,
+        // but mDispatch is untouched, this will result in a crash since in all likelihood, when mDelegate is set
+        // to nullptr, it can be assumed the derived mDispatch is also destroyed as a result, but not updated.
+
+        ChipLogProgress(ExchangeManager, "HandleMessage: %u, mDelegate is null for non Application exchanges. Setting dispatch to null", mExchangeId);
+        mDispatch = nullptr;
+    }
     // We hold a reference to the ExchangeContext here to
     // guard against Close() calls(decrementing the reference
     // count) by the protocol before the CHIP Exchange
@@ -427,8 +457,11 @@ CHIP_ERROR ExchangeContext::HandleMessage(const PacketHeader & packetHeader, con
         MessageHandled();
     });
 
-    ReturnErrorOnFailure(mDispatch->OnMessageReceived(packetHeader.GetFlags(), payloadHeader, packetHeader.GetMessageId(),
-                                                      peerAddress, msgFlags, GetReliableMessageContext()));
+    if (mDispatch != nullptr)
+    {
+        ReturnErrorOnFailure(mDispatch->OnMessageReceived(packetHeader.GetFlags(), payloadHeader, packetHeader.GetMessageId(),
+                                                          peerAddress, msgFlags, GetReliableMessageContext()));
+    }
 
     if (IsAckPending() && !mDelegate)
     {
diff --git a/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.h b/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.h
index a70b2bfcb..71b7e3909 100644
--- a/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.h
+++ b/src/messaging/ExchangeContext.h
@@ -184,6 +184,7 @@ public:
 private:
     Timeout mResponseTimeout       = 0; // Maximum time to wait for response (in milliseconds); 0 disables response timeout.
     ExchangeDelegate * mDelegate   = nullptr;
+    bool mDelegateDefaultedToApplicationExchangeDispatch = false;
     ExchangeManager * mExchangeMgr = nullptr;
     ExchangeACL * mExchangeACL     = nullptr;

```

This was most recently tested when applied to this rev:

```
commit ab665728d96a946ef3d6e2715784b7dbc0f6a3eb
Author: Andrei Litvin <andrei@andy314.com>
Date:   Sat Aug 21 10:49:52 2021 -0400
```

 #### Problem
The code looks like this:
```
    uint8_t buf[read_size];
    ReturnErrorOnFailure(mStorage.ReadValueBin(key, buf, read_size, read_size));

    size_t copy_size = std::min(value_size, read_size - offset_bytes);
...
    ::memcpy(value, buf + offset_bytes, copy_size);
```

What happens when `offset_bytes > read_size`?  Looks to me like we will happily start reading at past the end of the buffer and probably read `value_size` bytes from there.

 #### Proposed Solution
Probably need to return an error if `offset_bytes > read_size`.

@user_00080 @user_00011

#### Problem

In `IPEndPointBasis::PostPacketBufferEvent()` for LwIP we have:
```
const CHIP_ERROR error = aLayer->PostEvent(aTarget, aEventType, (uintptr_t) System::LwIPPacketBufferView::UnsafeGetLwIPpbuf(aBuffer));
if (error == CHIP_NO_ERROR)
{
    // If PostEvent() succeeded, it has ownership of the buffer, so we need to release it (without freeing it).
    static_cast<void>(std::move(aBuffer).UnsafeRelease());
}
```

However, `PostEvent()` does:
```
PlatformMgr().PostEvent(&event);
return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
```
because platform `_PostEvent()` returns `void`.

So, if the platform actually fails to deliver the event, that is not reported, and the packet buffer leaks. 

#### Proposed Solution

Return and propagate status from platform  `_PostEvent()`.

