body:
 #### Problem
I have intermittently seen hangs in TestInetEndPoint, and I finally had the time to attach a debugger to one.

We're in `TestResolveHostAddress` looping forever in this loop:
```
        while (!callbackHandlerCalled)
        {
            ServiceNetwork(kSleepTimeMilliseconds);
        }
```
on lines 146-149.  That's the wait for results after calling:
```
    err = gInet.ResolveHostAddress(testHostName1, 1, testDestAddr, HandleDNSResolveComplete, &callbackHandlerCalled);
```
where `testHostName1` is `"www.google.com"`.

 #### Proposed Solution
What I don't really know is how we got into this state where the callback is just not getting called....

@user_00041 @user_00002 @user_00001 @user_00225 @user_00011

 #### Problem
When a subscription is considered terminated after the schedule report exceeds the Maximum Interval Ceiling, the Callback that is fired (`OnLivenessTimeoutCallback`) results in a crash/abort. To reproduce this issue, establish a Subscription with a Node, and turn off the Node (Publisher) to stop the Node(Publisher) from reporting which should result in the Liveness Time to trigger once it exceeds the Maximum Interval Ceiling. 

`ReadClient::OnLivenessTimeoutCallback()` will attempt to propagate the timeout to the delegate (IM Engine) which results in an Abort when attempting to get the Secure Session of the ReadClient's Exchange Context in the following function that Gets Call Through (`OnLivenessTimeoutCallback` -> `ReadClient::ShutdownInternal()` -> `mpDelegate->ReadError()` which is the following method

```
CHIP_ERROR DeviceControllerInteractionModelDelegate::ReadError(const app::ReadClient * apReadClient, CHIP_ERROR aError)
{
    app::ClusterInfo path;
    path.mNodeId = apReadClient->GetExchangeContext()->GetSecureSession().GetPeerNodeId();
    IMReadReportAttributesResponseCallback(apReadClient, path, nullptr, Protocols::InteractionModel::Status::Failure);
    return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
}
```

I think the underlying problem is that Read Client is persisting the Exchange Context `mExchangeCtx` for the Report Exchanges initiated by the Publisher for the schedule reports, these Exchanges will be destructed once the Report is acknowledged by the Node. The current crash is one symptom of the underlying issue.

The issue is observed with the `test_event_6` branch (SHA: `005ce592e74e6194ecc19fd4da08cd403b3be05d`)

 #### Proposed Solution

@user_00012 If I understand correctly, `ProcessReportData` will trigger the transmission of a StatusResponse, which is the final message in this exchange, resulting in the closure of the exchange automatically.

So this setting of the `mpExchangeCtx` to nullptr is purely for cleanliness, and serves no functional value right?

_Originally posted by @user_00018 in https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/10061#discussion_r718804563_

During the write interaction, If the session expires leading to closing the associated exchanges, the session value is cleared before the exchange context is removed. 

```cpp
void ExchangeContext::OnConnectionExpired()
{
    // Reset our mSecureSession to a default-initialized (hence not matching any
    // connection state) value, because it's still referencing the now-expired
    // connection.  This will mean that no more messages can be sent via this
    // exchange, which seems fine given the semantics of connection expiration.
    mSecureSession.ClearValue();
```

However, this can potentially lead to crash during WriteError which uses this exchange context and session value out of it to determine the nodeId in propagating the failure callback. For instance, `DeviceControllerInteractionModelDelegate::WriteResponseError` proceeds to the following
```cpp
bool IMWriteResponseCallback(const chip::app::WriteClient * writeClient, EmberAfStatus status)
{
    ChipLogProgress(Zcl, "WriteResponse:");
    LogStatus(status);

    Callback::Cancelable * onSuccessCallback = nullptr;
    Callback::Cancelable * onFailureCallback = nullptr;
    NodeId sourceNodeId                      = writeClient->GetSourceNodeId();
...
```
Where the WriteClient will attempt to retrieve the nodeId using the evicted session handle:
```cpp
    NodeId GetSourceNodeId() const
    {
        return mpExchangeCtx != nullptr ? mpExchangeCtx->GetSecureSession().GetPeerNodeId() : kUndefinedNodeId;
    }
```
It's similar to Issue #10047 but occurs during Write or Other Interactions that access the values from evicted exchangeContext or sessionHandle. It can be reproduced by calling `SecureSessionMgr::ExpirePairing` during such interaction.

 #### Problem
The generated code looks like this:
```
CHIP_ERROR Type::Decode(TLV::TLVReader &reader) {
    CHIP_ERROR err;
    TLV::TLVType outer;
    err = reader.EnterContainer(outer);
    ReturnErrorOnFailure(err);
    while ((err = reader.Next()) == CHIP_NO_ERROR) {
        switch (chip::TLV::TagNumFromTag(reader.GetTag()))
```
but this is assuming the tag is in fact a context-specific tag.  Nothing has checked that it is!  If it's not (i.e. a profile tag, though someone _could_ send bogus data with a struct containing anonymous tags), then behavior of `TagNumFromTag` is wrong, in that it will return part of the profile tag, which could match the value of one of our field ids even though the tag is not the right one.

 #### Proposed Solution
Either check that the tag is a context tag or do compares on tags, not tag numbers.  Add tests.

@user_00030 @user_00018

 #### Problem
The code looks like this:
```
    uint64_t extensionTagNum = TagNumFromTag(reader.GetTag());
    OID extensionOID;

    if (extensionTagNum == kTag_FutureExtension)
    {
...
    }
    else
    {
...
            extensionOID = GetOID(kOIDCategory_Extension, static_cast<uint8_t>(extensionTagNum));
```
But nothing checks that the tag is a context tag, or a profiile tag, so TagNumFromTag could be returning complete garbage here, or could be returning things that are not context-specific tag numbers.

And either way it could be returning things that don't fit in `uint8_t`.

 #### Proposed Solution
Sort out what should actually be going on here to make this safe.  Add tests.

@user_00010

 #### Problem
In SetupPayload.cpp we have:
```
bool IsCHIPTag(uint8_t tag)
{
    return tag >= (1 << kRawVendorTagLengthInBits);
}

bool IsVendorTag(uint8_t tag)
{
   return tag < (1 << kRawVendorTagLengthInBits);
}
```

but in the spec "5.1.4.1. Manufacturer-specific Elements" says:

> Manufacturer-specific elements SHALL be tagged with context-specific tags that have semantics which are defined by the vendor for use in the products using their Vendor ID, and SHALL use tag values 0x80 to 0xFF.  Tag values 0x00 to 0x7F are reserved to indicate CHIP-common elements.
    
which seems exactly backwards from the code.

What exactly is going on here?  @user_00006 

 #### Proposed Solution
Sort out what's going on, and fix or document to make it clear how this code and the spec interact.  Add tests as needed.

