[2021-10-20T15:33:28Z] user_01470: For iterating, something like a search context class has proven useful. This is an example of how it would be used:

```
void CZigBeeClusterGPSink::LoadSinkTable()
{
	CZigBeeGPStorageItem item;

	item.m_nType = CZigBeeGPStorageItem::sinkTableEntry;

	// Clear existing sink table entries
	m_gpds.clear();

	// Find all existing sink table entries
	CPersistentStorageSearch search(theStorage, item, sizeof(item) - 1);

	// Add persistently stored records to the sink table in memory
	while (search.FindNext(item, sizeof(item)))
	{
		CZigBeeGPSinkTableEntry entry;
		CZigBeeAddress gpd;
		CPacket raw(item.GetValueSize());

		raw.SetSize(item.GetValueSize());

		// Load item value from storage. This is a sink table entry in
		// standard "over-the-air" format
		if (search.Load(raw.GetData(), raw.GetSize()) && entry.Load(raw, gpd))
		{
			m_gpds[gpd] = entry;

			// Notify the stub that updated device information is available
			stub.UpdateDeviceInformation(gpd, entry.m_info);
		}
		else
		{
			_TRACE1(c7b::zcl, "GPS: failed to load sink table entry #%u\r\n",
				item.m_nIndex);
		}
	}
}
```

In above example, a search key that is shorter than a fully qualified key could potentially yield multiple results. Which is how you exploit the key hierarchy.   

