[2021-11-11T05:12:54Z] user_00018: > I'm not sure I'm following this part.

When you call `AttributeValueEncoder::EncodeList` today, it calls into `TagBoundEncoder::EncodeList` to actually do the starting of the list, then, calling the lambda with a reference to the `TagBoundEncoder` passed in. However, this is likely not the object we should pass in to handle this list chunking logic, since `TagBoundEncoder` has a fairly narrow, well-defined purpose.

What should be passed into the lambda is a reference to the `AttributeValueEncoder` itself, which needs to house a `EncodeListItem` method on it that will in turn under the covers, manage the chunking logic, only calling `DataModel::Encode` when it needs to.

Something like this in AttributeValueEncoder:

```
    template <typename... Ts>
    CHIP_ERROR EncodeListItem(Ts... aArgs) const
    {
        if (noMoreSpace) {
            return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
        }
  
        mCurrentListIndex++;

        // If we already wrote out a bunch of items in a previous encode session,
        // wait till we catch up to that index.
        if (mCurrentListIndex < mLastWrittenListIndex) {
           return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
        }

        if (mListIndex == 0) {
            // Encode empty AttributeData IB to start things off.
        }
        
        // Start an Attribute Data IB
        Encode(*mWriter, mTag, std::forward<Ts>(aArgs)...);
        // Close out Attribute Data IB
    }

    template <typename ListGenerator>
    CHIP_ERROR EncodeList(ListGenerator aCallback)
    {
        mTriedEncode = true;
        if (mWriter == nullptr)
        {
            return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
        }

        aCallback(this);
    }
```

