 #### Problem

In the generated cluster code (e.g. `NetworkCommissioningClient-InvokeSubscribeImpl.cpp`), an `Optional<Long>` is passed as an argument for the `breadcrumb` attribute for the generated cluster commands (e.g. `addOrUpdateWiFiNetwork`).

The generated code looks like:

```
if (breadcrumb != nullptr) {
    jobject optionalValue_0;
    chip::JniReferences::GetInstance().GetOptionalValue(breadcrumb, optionalValue_0);
    auto & definedValue_0 = request.breadcrumb.Emplace();
    definedValue_0 = static_cast<std::remove_reference_t<decltype(definedValue_0)>>(chip::JniReferences::GetInstance().LongToPrimitive(optionalValue_0));
}
```

However, this fails with a JNI error on the call to `LongToPrimitive`, as the `optionalValue_0` (the unwrapped value from the `Optional`) may be null.

The generating code is here:
https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/blob/master/scripts/idl/generators/java/ChipClustersCpp.jinja

 #### Proposed Solution

The generated code should probably look like:


```
if (breadcrumb != nullptr) {
    jobject optionalValue_0;
    chip::JniReferences::GetInstance().GetOptionalValue(breadcrumb, optionalValue_0);
    if (optionalValue_0 != nullptr) {
        auto & definedValue_0 = request.breadcrumb.Emplace();
        definedValue_0 = static_cast<std::remove_reference_t<decltype(definedValue_0)>>(chip::JniReferences::GetInstance().LongToPrimitive(optionalValue_0));
    }
}
```

 #### Problem
I am trying to read attribute **update-state-progress** during OTA, but the device blocks some time after receiving the read request. From my analysis, it seems that there is a retransmission timer that is started with delay 0ms. This means the **ReliableMessageMgr::Timeout** is called without delay and is not expecting that, since the assert **VerifyOrDie(!entry->retainedBuf.IsNull())** fails. 
This lambda function from **src/messaging/ReliableMessageMgr.cpp**, line 339, sets the **nextWakeTime** to the min value of all nextRetransTime from the retransmission table entries:
```
mRetransTable.ForEachActiveObject([&](auto * entry) {
        if (entry->nextRetransTime < nextWakeTime)
        {
            nextWakeTime = entry->nextRetransTime;
            ChipLogDetail(ExchangeManager, "nextWakeTime set to nextRetransTime %" PRIu64 "ms", nextWakeTime.count());
        }
        return Loop::Continue;
    });
```
In my case, I saw that at least one of the nextRetransTime values is 0. Is a value of 0 for retransmission time an actual use case? 
Looking at the specification, I see in section **4.11.8. Parameters and Constants** the default values for MRP_RETRY_INTERVAL_IDLE/ACTIVE are set to 300ms.

This is the stack trace:
![StackTrace](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/102153746/167863121-4ea8bb82-5d14-4af5-9d4e-9915418160da.png)

This is the exact line (121) where the assert fails:
![Assert](https://user-images.githubusercontent.com/102153746/167863434-b289d858-f5f1-4c02-9d0e-6eae5c86b4ab.png)

I have added some logging messages to print some time values. Also, I've added some logging to see the values of nextRetransTime and retainedBuf when AddToRetransTable is called. This is the .diff file with my modifications:
[ReliableMessageMgrDiff.txt](https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/files/8670416/ReliableMessageMgrDiff.txt)

I will also attach the chip-tool and the device log files. Please note that the device log file has some tokenized strings that could not be detokenized:
[chip-tool.txt](https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/files/8670423/chip-tool.txt)
[device.txt](https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/files/8670424/device.txt)

 #### Proposed Solution
I have not found a solution yet, since I'm not sure about the retransmission time question formulated above.
If I enhance the lambda function **if** to check that **entry->nextRetransTime** is also different than 0, then the device will never block if it receives a read request:
```
if (entry->nextRetransTime != System::Clock::Timestamp::zero() && entry->nextRetransTime < nextWakeTime)
```
This was just a quick way for me to confirm that the nextRetransTime set to 0 was causing the actual issue.
Please let me know if you need more details.

 #### Problem
As far as I can tell, we don't properly reserve one read handler _per fabric_.  What we do is:

If a subscribe comes in, and the number of read handlers (so active reads/subscribes) allocated is more than (total available - number of fabrics) we start evicting existing subscriptions.  As a simple example, if MAX_FABRICS were 2 and we supported 3 subscriptions + 1 read per fabric, we would have a cap of 6 subscriptions in `EnsureResourceForSubscription`.  If we have 2 subscriptions on fabric 1, 2 subscriptions on fabric 2, and 2 reads in progress, an attempt to establish a new subscription will evict one of the existing ones.

Worse yet, if we have 3 subscriptions on fabric 1, 2 subscriptions on fabric 2, and 2 reads in progress (total of 7), an attempt to establish a new subscription on fabric 2 will evict _both_ existing subscriptions even though we could have just established our new subscription without evicting anything.

That also means that this block of code in `InteractionModelEngine::OnReadInitialRequest`:
```
    // Reserve the last ReadHandler for ReadInteraction
    if (aInteractionType == ReadHandler::InteractionType::Subscribe && ((handlerPoolCapacity - GetNumActiveReadHandlers()) == 1) &&
        !HasActiveRead())
    {
        ChipLogDetail(InteractionModel, "Reserve the last ReadHandler for IM read Interaction");
        aStatus = Protocols::InteractionModel::Status::ResourceExhausted;
        return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
    }
```
is dead code, because after we finish `EnsureResourceForSubscription` we will always have MAX_FABRICS available read handlers, as far as I can tell.

 #### Proposed Solution
1. Fix logic in `EnsureResourceForSubscription`.  Probably remove the code in `OnReadInitialRequest`, because `EnsureResourceForSubscription` should handle that.  
2. Add tests that would catch this, if we can.  Would involve chunked reads that are still in progress when a subscription request comes in; should be doable with wildcard reads, perhaps.

@user_00013 @user_00018

