[2022-10-17T14:05:47Z] user_00012: The app level solution is the right one for now.  Figuring out a batching/deferral strategy for NVM attributes is something that did not happen for 1.0 but might happen as we do follow-up work; it was not clear whether there is a reasonable default strategy that would work for most cases well (because any deferral strategy runs the risk of data loss, etc, and the tradeoffs there might be very application-specific).

body:
If a Light Bulb device is controlled remotely by a Dimmer Switch, and the CurrentLevel attribute of LevelControl cluster has NVM storage type, the attribute may be written to flash pretty frequently, which may greatly contribute to the rapid flash wearout. The issue was spotted by @user_00024.

It may be needed to support the attribute persistence differently for the class of attributes whose writes can be deferred without risking serious issues.

**Possible solutions**
1. Handle that at the application level by inheriting from `DefaultAttributePersistenceProvider` and overriding its `WriteValue` method as presented in the pseudocode below
```
CHIP_ERROR MyAttributePersistenceProvider::WriteValue(const ConcreteAttributePath & aPath,
                                                           const EmberAfAttributeMetadata * aMetadata, const ByteSpan & aValue)
{
    if (IsDeferredWriteAttribute(aPath))
    {
        if (AddOrUpdateWriteBuffer(aPath, aValue) == CHIP_NO_ERROR)
        {
            StartTimer(1000ms, FlushWriteBuffer);
            return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
        }
    }

    return DefaultAttributePersistenceProvider::WriteValue(aPath, aMetadata, aValue);
}
```
2. Handle that at the Matter stack level by introducing another storage type, e.g. NVM Deferred, and provide a standard `AttributePersistenceProvider` decorator class that handles deferred writes.

