It can be solved by direct include host/ble_hs.h in /examples/platform/esp32/common/CommonDeviceCallbacks.cpp

```
#define min
#define max
#include "host/ble_hs.h"
#undef min
#undef max
```


Looks like a bug

### Bug prevalence

Every time during compilation

### GitHub hash of the SDK that was being used

V1.0.0.1

### Platform

esp32

### Platform Version(s)

4.4.2

### Anything else?

_No response_

### Feature description

We are working on a matter controller device.

We need to stop the Matter stack properly to test our code with Valgrind to make sure that there is no memory leak. 

Currently it's difficult because the Matter stack is not able to be stopped properly.

It should be good to implement something that permit to cleanup all the memory used by the stack when we stop the stack.

Also, what is the good way to stop the stack ?

```
    // chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().HandleServerShuttingDown();
    // DeviceControllerFactory::GetInstance().ReleaseSystemState();
    // DeviceControllerFactory::GetInstance().Shutdown();
    // ReturnLogErrorOnFailure(chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().StopEventLoopTask());

    // chip::Platform::MemoryShutdown();
    chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().StopEventLoopTask();
    // chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().Shutdown();

    // Do this after StopEventLoopTask() so we don't need to Lock/Unlock the stack
    for (auto it = mCommissioners.begin(); it != mCommissioners.end(); it++) {
        ShutdownCommissioner(it->first);
    }
```

### Platform

other

### Platform Version(s)

_No response_

### Anything else?

_No response_

```
src/platform/Linux/NetworkCommissioningWiFiDriver.cpp:    ChipLogProgress(NetworkProvisioning, "LinuxNetworkCommissioningDelegate: SSID: %s",
src/platform/nxp/mw320/NetworkCommissioningWiFiDriver.cpp:    ChipLogProgress(NetworkProvisioning, "LinuxNetworkCommissioningDelegate: SSID: %s", networkId.data());
src/platform/webos/NetworkCommissioningWiFiDriver.cpp:    ChipLogProgress(NetworkProvisioning, "LinuxNetworkCommissioningDelegate: SSID: %s", networkId.data());
```

all of those are logging a ByteSpan's pointer as `%s` but there is no reason the data woul be null-terminated.  This can lead to crashes and other bad things.

comments:
[2023-01-21T15:40:09Z] user_00012: OK, so given these decls:
```
typedef void (^TestCallback)(NSError * _Nullable error);

- (void)testMethod:(BOOL)arg completion:(TestCallback)completion;
- (void)testMethod:(BOOL)arg completionHandler:(TestCallback)completionHandler /*NS_SWIFT_UNAVAILABLE("")*/ /*NS_SWIFT_DISABLE_ASYNC*/;
```
and this Swift code:
```
    testObj.testMethod(false) { error in }
    testObj.testMethod(false, completion: { error in })
    testObj.testMethod(false, completionHandler: { error in })
    do {
        try await testObj.testMethod(false)
    } catch {
    }
```
calls 1 and 4 error out with "Ambiguous use of 'testMethod'" and items 2 and 3 are fine (but warn about "Consider using asynchronous alternative function").  This is where we are right now.

If I uncomment the `NS_SWIFT_DISABLE_ASYNC`, then calls 3 and 4 are completely fine, call 2 is fine but has that "alternative function" warning, and call 1 errors out.

If I instead uncomment the `NS_SWIFT_UNAVAILABLE`, then call 3 errors out, calls 1 and 2 are fine but have the warning about "alternative function", and call 4 is fine.

There does not seem to be a way to make all four calls work, unfortunately.  Given how Swift is usually written, we're going to take the "make call 3 not work and everything else work" option, so add `NS_SWIFT_UNAVAILABLE` in all the cases where we have two selectors that differ only in `completion` vs `completionHandler`.

