### Type

Spec Compliance Issue

### Anything else?

_No response_

Publisher subscription maximal interval is currently limited at 3600s (1h) as per spec:
// https://github.com/CHIP-Specifications/connectedhomeip-spec/blob/61a9d19e6af12fdfb0872bcff26d19de6c680a1a/src/Ch02_Architecture.adoc#1122-subscribe-interaction-limits

This needs to be updated in accordance with spec changes for ICDs.

### Feature description

In https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/26566 we ran into an interesting issue, due to code that did this, in an `AttributeAccessInterface::Read` override:
```
    if (TemperatureControl::Attributes::SupportedTemperatureLevels::Id == aPath.mAttributeId)
    {
...
        CHIP_ERROR err;
        err = aEncoder.EncodeList([&](const auto & encoder) -> CHIP_ERROR {
...
        });
    }
    return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
```

and failed to propagate the return value from EncodeList out.  This resulted in us starting a new `AttributeReportIB`, getting as far as encoding the control and tag for the `AttributePathIB`, then running out of buffer.  Since the return value did not get propagated, we did not roll back the partially encoded `AttributeReportIB`, and then when we finished out our message there were un-closed containers (the close-containers we did for the `AttributeReports` array and the `ReportData` message closed the `AttributePathIB` and `AttributeReportIB` respectively, and we ended up with not enough close containers, what looked like an empty path garbage bytes for the value, etc.  Luckily something failed on decoding that.

I _think_ that instead of relying on callees to propagate errors, `AttributeValueEncoder` should just keep track of all errors it knows about.  This in particular includes all errors due to calls on the `TLVWriter`, since it controls all those calls and the AttributeAccessInterface implementation cannot write TLV directly.  Then we ensure that if any of that errored out we return that error to the reporting engine.

The other thing we should consider doing is moving the responsibility for rolling back partial IBs out of the reporting engine and into the code that instantiates the `AttributeValueEncoder` or talks to the ZAP attribute store in ReadSingleClusterData, and make the "we ran out of buffer, start chunking" contract more explicit at that level, instead of relying on magic error values that could happen for reasons that have nothing to do with running out of buffer.

