body:
#### Description
When the ICD enters ActiveMode, we determine the ActiveMode timer based on the ActiveModeDuration and if we have any reqs to keep us in ActiveMode.

```
if (activeModeDuration == kZero && !mKeepActiveFlags.HasAny())
{
       // Network Activity triggered the active mode and activeModeDuration is 0.
       // Stay active for at least Active Mode Threshold.
      activeModeDuration = ICDConfigurationData::GetInstance().GetActiveModeThreshold();
}
```

If we have ActiveMode req, we leave the ActiveModeDuration to 0. This is problematic because the `OnTransitionToIdle` ends up being called immediatly.

```
Milliseconds32 activeModeJitterInterval = Milliseconds32(ICD_ACTIVE_TIME_JITTER_MS);
activeModeJitterInterval =
    (activeModeDuration >= activeModeJitterInterval) ? activeModeDuration - activeModeJitterInterval : kZero;
    
DeviceLayer::SystemLayer().StartTimer(activeModeJitterInterval, OnTransitionToIdle, this);
```

#### Description
When we have a req and ActiveModeDuration is 0, we do not update the ActiveMode timer duration with the ActiveModeThreshold when transitionning from Idle to Active mode.

```
if (activeModeDuration == kZero && !mKeepActiveFlags.HasAny())
{
    // Network Activity triggered the active mode and activeModeDuration is 0.
    // Stay active for at least Active Mode Threshold.
    activeModeDuration = ICDConfigurationData::GetInstance().GetActiveModeThreshold();
}
```

Since a group message doesn't expect and ack or response, we immediatly go to IdleMode once the message is sent since the ExchangeContext is destroyed.

We need to adapt the Req logic for it not to be added when we create a Group Exchange Context.

