body:
### Build issue(s)

The project fails to compile with a C++20-compliant compiler. The failure occurs due to the following issues:

Ambiguity in resolving the operator== call between ConcreteAttributePath and ConcreteDataAttributePath.
1. `ChunkedWriteCallback::IsAppendingToLastItem` (ChunkedWriteCallback.cpp)
2. `IsSameAttribute` (WriteHandler.cpp)

Compound assignment to volatile.
1. `diff |= x ^ y;` (CHIPCryptoPALPSA.cpp)

Resolving these issues is straightforward and results in a successful build.
```
diff --git src/app/ChunkedWriteCallback.cpp src/app/ChunkedWriteCallback.cpp
index eccd2b6049..6ec1d07b6f 100644
--- src/app/ChunkedWriteCallback.cpp
+++ src/app/ChunkedWriteCallback.cpp
@@ -81,7 +81,7 @@ bool ChunkedWriteCallback::IsAppendingToLastItem(const ConcreteDataAttributePath
     {
         return false;
     }
-    if (!mProcessingAttributePath.HasValue() || !(mProcessingAttributePath.Value() == aPath))
+    if (!mProcessingAttributePath.HasValue() || !(mProcessingAttributePath.Value() == static_cast<ConcreteAttributePath>(aPath)))
     {
         return false;
     }
diff --git src/app/WriteHandler.cpp src/app/WriteHandler.cpp
index fbcab6bcbe..38bb834df9 100644
--- src/app/WriteHandler.cpp
+++ src/app/WriteHandler.cpp
@@ -323,7 +323,7 @@ namespace {
 // To reduce the various use of previousProcessed.HasValue() && previousProcessed.Value() == nextAttribute to save code size.
 bool IsSameAttribute(const Optional<ConcreteAttributePath> & previousProcessed, const ConcreteDataAttributePath & nextAttribute)
 {
-    return previousProcessed.HasValue() && previousProcessed.Value() == nextAttribute;
+    return previousProcessed.HasValue() && previousProcessed.Value() == static_cast<ConcreteAttributePath>(nextAttribute);
 }

 bool ShouldReportListWriteEnd(const Optional<ConcreteAttributePath> & previousProcessed, bool previousProcessedAttributeIsList,
diff --git src/crypto/CHIPCryptoPALPSA.cpp src/crypto/CHIPCryptoPALPSA.cpp
index 2f410a29de..ea66e023d3 100644
--- src/crypto/CHIPCryptoPALPSA.cpp
+++ src/crypto/CHIPCryptoPALPSA.cpp
@@ -777,7 +777,7 @@ int mbedtls_ct_memcmp_copy(const void * a, const void * b, size_t n)
          * This avoids IAR compiler warning:
          * 'the order of volatile accesses is undefined ..' */
         unsigned char x = A[i], y = B[i];
-        diff |= x ^ y;
+        diff = diff | (x ^ y);
     }

     return ((int) diff);
```

### Platform

nrf connect, all

### Anything else?

I only tested nrf connect but I think it is safe to assume all platforms are affected. I was not able to identify any similar instances to the above that may cause build failures in other projects.

- a Follow-up to https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/40728 which fixed the memory leak https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/issues/40737


- Basically when starting a Linux App that is already commissioned, the following lines made us call `ConnectivityManagerImpl::_WiFiPAFCancelPublish`, 
- We probably shouldn't need to call WiFiPAFShutdown on App start, and maybe just not Start Publishing PAF frames in the first place.

```cpp
// in Line examples/platform/linux/AppMain.cpp:932
    if (Server::GetInstance().GetFabricTable().FabricCount() != 0)
    {
        ChipLogProgress(AppServer, "Fabric already commissioned. Canceling publishing");
        DeviceLayer::ConnectivityMgr().WiFiPAFShutdown(LinuxDeviceOptions::GetInstance().mPublishId,
                                                       chip::WiFiPAF::WiFiPafRole::kWiFiPafRole_Publisher);
    }
```

- @user_01397 has some suggestions such as moving the WiFI PAF methods to `CommissioningWindowManager::StartAdvertisement()`

### Reproduction steps

Any change in fabrics (add /remove) immediately followed by the power loss is causing loss of all fabrics (older ones as well.)

1. Add fabric / fabrics
2. Wait for some time (to ensure fabric is properly written to persistent storage) 
3. Add another fabric
4. Power off the device (ungracefully) simulating sudden power loss. 
5. After power on, note all the fabrics are lost. 

The issue is because of the following code : 

/src/platform/Linux/CHIPLinuxStorageIni.cpp

`CHIP_ERROR ChipLinuxStorageIni::CommitConfig(const std::string & configFile)
{
    CHIP_ERROR retval   = CHIP_NO_ERROR;
    std::string tmpPath = configFile + "-XXXXXX";

    int fd = mkstemp(&tmpPath[0]);
    if (fd != -1)
    {
        std::ofstream ofs;

        ChipLogProgress(DeviceLayer, "writing settings to file (%s)", tmpPath.c_str());

        ofs.open(tmpPath, std::ofstream::out | std::ofstream::trunc);
        mConfigStore.generate(ofs);

        close(fd);

        if (rename(tmpPath.c_str(), configFile.c_str()) == 0)
        {
            ChipLogProgress(DeviceLayer, "renamed tmp file to file (%s)", configFile.c_str());
        }
        else
        {
            ChipLogError(DeviceLayer, "failed to rename (%s), %s (%d)", tmpPath.c_str(), strerror(errno), errno);
            retval = CHIP_ERROR_WRITE_FAILED;
        }
    }
    else
    {
        ChipLogError(DeviceLayer, "failed to open file (%s) for writing", tmpPath.c_str());
        retval = CHIP_ERROR_OPEN_FAILED;
    }

    return retval;
}`


The ofstream is not flushed / closed before closing the fd. So any buffer which is still in user-space may have not be written to the kernel and also kernel's page cache is not yet written to disk. So fix this : 

`CHIP_ERROR ChipLinuxStorageIni::CommitConfig(const std::string & configFile)
{
    CHIP_ERROR retval   = CHIP_NO_ERROR;
    std::string tmpPath = configFile + "-XXXXXX";

    int fd = mkstemp(&tmpPath[0]);
    if (fd != -1)
    {
        std::ofstream ofs;

        ChipLogProgress(DeviceLayer, "writing settings to file (%s)", tmpPath.c_str());

        ofs.open(tmpPath, std::ofstream::out | std::ofstream::trunc);
        mConfigStore.generate(ofs);

        ofs.close()  // Flush user-space buffers
        fsync(fd)    // Flush kernel buffers to disk
        close(fd);

        if (rename(tmpPath.c_str(), configFile.c_str()) == 0)
        {
            ChipLogProgress(DeviceLayer, "renamed tmp file to file (%s)", configFile.c_str());
        }
        else
        {
            ChipLogError(DeviceLayer, "failed to rename (%s), %s (%d)", tmpPath.c_str(), strerror(errno), errno);
            retval = CHIP_ERROR_WRITE_FAILED;
        }
    }
    else
    {
        ChipLogError(DeviceLayer, "failed to open file (%s) for writing", tmpPath.c_str());
        retval = CHIP_ERROR_OPEN_FAILED;
    }

    return retval;
}`

### Bug prevalence

Everytime when I try this sequence

