[2025-09-14T04:36:16Z] user_00012: @user_00224 Is this about TLS or TLV?

You can have a "streaming" TLVWriter with small current buffers if you give it an appropriate TLVBackingStore implementation to write to, no?

---

[2025-09-15T15:10:08Z] user_00224: Possibly, I did not go to look at the `TLVWriter` impl; my concern was simply that this seems to be done throughout where `Init(uint8_t * buf, size_t maxLen)` is called, perhaps because it is the most straightforward way of doing so? It's a common pattern throughout, "just take up a chunk of memory on the stack", and I have a feeling it contributes to non-trivial amounts of memory waste

A simpler paradigm/pattern could help, i.e. if it's simple & straightforward to setup streaming TLV write & that's followed throughout, devs may not fallback to `Init(uint8_t * buf, size_t maxLen)`

---

[2025-09-15T16:10:55Z] user_00224: https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/40977 is another good example - why do we have to allocate a huge chunk of a buffer instead of supporting streaming here? (which I had kind of assumed chained buffers were)

This again seems like it'd take more memory than we need - streaming would allow us to allocate exactly what's necessary

---

[2025-09-15T16:23:27Z] user_00224: Interestingly, @user_00018 seems to have run into this with https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/pull/11705, with effectively a "TODO" that ~mirrors this issue.

It seems that, in general, we're just allocating/copying large buffers throughout instead of using smaller ephemeral buffers.

```
    //
    // To generate the final reconstituted list, we need to allocate a contiguous
    // buffer than can hold the entirety of its contents. To do so, we need to figure out
    // how big a buffer to allocate. This requires walking the buffered list items and computing their TLV sizes,
    // summing them all up and adding a bit of slop to account for the TLV array the list elements will go into.
    //
    // The alternative was to use a PacketBufferTLVWriter backed by chained packet buffers to
    // write out the list - this would have removed the need for this first pass. However,
    // we cannot actually back a TLVReader with a chained buffer since that violates the ability
    // for us to create readers off-of readers. Each reader would assume exclusive ownership of the chained
    // buffer and mutate the state within TLVPacketBufferBackingStore, preventing shared use.
    //
    // To avoid that, a single contiguous buffer is the best likely approach for now.
    //
```

---

[2025-09-15T18:01:30Z] user_00012: So specifically in the IM context: You can't (currently?) do streaming for read or write any time you might want rewinding.

For IM TLVWriter: that means that anything that involves rolling back and chunking on running out of space (e.g. attribute reads) cannot use streaming and has to have a single-chunk buffer to work with.

For IM TLVReader: because the various IM decoding machinery assumes you can decode the same thing more than once as needed (true for lists to determine their length, true for various actions because we could up paths before we actually process the action, etc, etc), anything that will do IM decoding from a TLVReader generally needs that TLVReader to be backed by a continuous buffer.

Basically, any codepath that uses a copy constructor or assignment operator on a TLVReader/TLVWriter, or uses `Init(const TLVReader & aReader)` on TLVReader currently has to use a single contiguous buffer.

The comment cited from #11705 falls into that second (IM TLVReader) bucket, as the comment alludes to.  Same for #40614.

For other use cases, it's possible the existing TLVBackingStore might work, depending on the situation.

