# [Java] Missing thread-safe execution methods - cluster commands crash when called from non-Matter threads

## Summary

The Java bindings for Matter SDK lack critical thread-safety utilities that exist in the Python SDK, forcing developers to implement workarounds or risk crashes when calling cluster commands from non-Matter threads.

## Problem Description

### Python SDK (Works correctly)

Python provides built-in thread-safe execution:

```python
# Python has thread-safe execution built-in
await devCtrl.SendCommand(nodeId, endpoint, Clusters.OnOff.Commands.On())
# Automatically executes in Matter event loop thread
```

### Java SDK (Crashes!)

```java
// Java - NO thread safety!
ChipClusters.OnOffCluster cluster = new ChipClusters.OnOffCluster(devicePtr, endpoint);
cluster.on(callback);  // ❌ CRASHES if not called from Matter thread!
```

## Root Cause Analysis

### 1. ChipDeviceController methods ARE thread-safe

```cpp
// CHIPDeviceController-JNI.cpp
JNI_METHOD(void, pairDeviceWithCode) {
    chip::DeviceLayer::StackLock lock;  // ✅ Thread-safe
    // ... safe to call from any thread
}
```

### 2. ChipInteractionClient methods are NOT thread-safe

```cpp
// MatterInteractionClient-JNI.cpp
JNI_METHOD(void, invoke) {
    // ❌ NO StackLock! Must be called from Matter thread or crashes
    CHIP_ERROR err = invoke(...);
}
```

### 3. Cluster commands use unsafe invoke() internally

```java
// ChipClusters.java (generated)
public void on(DefaultClusterCallback callback) {
    invoke(...);  // ❌ Requires Matter thread, but no protection!
}
```

## Impact - Crashes in Common Use Cases

### Crash #1: UI Thread
```java
// ❌ CRASH - called from UI thread
button.setOnClickListener(v -> {
    cluster.on(callback);  // Segmentation fault or assertion failure!
});
```

### Crash #2: Background Thread
```java
// ❌ CRASH - called from executor/thread pool
executor.execute(() -> {
    cluster.moveToLevel(callback, level);  // Assertion failure!
});
```

### Crash #3: Network Callback
```java
// ❌ CRASH - called from network callback thread
onNetworkResponse(() -> {
    cluster.off(callback);  // Crash!
});
```

## Current Workaround (Required Custom Code)

We had to implement `ChipThreadHelper` to work around this critical limitation:

### Custom JNI Code
```cpp
// ChipThreadHelper-JNI.cpp (custom implementation - NOT in SDK!)
JNI_HELPER_METHOD(jboolean, executeOnChipThread)
(JNIEnv* env, jclass clazz, jobject callback, jobject userData, jboolean oneShot) {
    // Create callback context and schedule on Matter thread
    chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().ScheduleWork(ChipThreadTaskRunner, context);
    return JNI_TRUE;
}
```

### Custom Java Wrapper
```java
// ChipThreadHelper.java (custom - NOT in SDK!)
public class ChipThreadHelper {
    public static void runOnChipThread(Runnable task) {
        executeOnChipThread(new ChipThreadCallback() {
            public void run(Object userData) {
                task.run();
            }
        }, null, true);
    }

    private static native boolean executeOnChipThread(
        ChipThreadCallback callback, Object userData, boolean oneShot
    );
}
```

### Usage (Every cluster command needs this!)
```java
// Now developers must wrap EVERY cluster command
ChipThreadHelper.runOnChipThread(() -> {
    cluster.on(callback);  // ✅ Finally safe
});
```

## Comparison: Java vs Python

| Feature | Python SDK | Java SDK | Status |
|---------|-----------|----------|--------|
| Thread-safe cluster commands | ✅ Built-in | ❌ Missing | **CRITICAL BUG** |
| Automatic thread marshaling | ✅ Yes (asyncio) | ❌ No | **CRITICAL BUG** |
| Developer needs workarounds | ❌ No | ✅ Yes (custom JNI) | **CRITICAL BUG** |
| Documentation mentions threading | ✅ Clear | ❌ None | **Documentation Gap** |

## Proposed Solutions

### Option 1: Add StackLock to ChipInteractionClient (Simplest)

```cpp
// MatterInteractionClient-JNI.cpp
JNI_METHOD(void, invoke) {
    chip::DeviceLayer::StackLock lock;  // Just add this line!
    CHIP_ERROR err = invoke(...);
}

JNI_METHOD(void, read) {
    chip::DeviceLayer::StackLock lock;  // And here
    CHIP_ERROR err = read(...);
}

JNI_METHOD(void, subscribe) {
    chip::DeviceLayer::StackLock lock;  // And here
    CHIP_ERROR err = subscribe(...);
}
```

**Pros:**
- Minimal code change
- Makes Java SDK consistent with ChipDeviceController methods
- No breaking changes
- Fixes all cluster commands automatically

**Cons:**
- May have slight performance overhead (but same as other methods)

### Option 2: Add executeOnChipThread to ChipDeviceController

```java
// ChipDeviceController.java
public void executeOnChipThread(Runnable task) {
    nativeExecuteOnChipThread(deviceControllerPtr, task);
}

private native void nativeExecuteOnChipThread(long handle, Runnable task);
```

```cpp
// CHIPDeviceController-JNI.cpp
JNI_METHOD(void, nativeExecuteOnChipThread)
(JNIEnv* env, jobject self, jlong handle, jobject task) {
    // Schedule on Matter thread
    chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().ScheduleWork([...] {
        // Call Java task.run()
    });
}
```

**Usage:**
```java
deviceController.executeOnChipThread(() -> {
    cluster.on(callback);
});
```

**Pros:**
- Explicit API for thread safety
- Developers have control

**Cons:**
- Requires manual wrapping
- Easy to forget

### Option 3: Async/Future-based API (Best Developer Experience)

```java
// ChipClusters.java (generated code changes)
public CompletableFuture<Void> onAsync() {
    CompletableFuture<Void> future = new CompletableFuture<>();
    executeOnChipThread(() -> {
        on(new DefaultClusterCallback() {
            @Override
            public void onSuccess() {
                future.complete(null);
            }

            @Override
            public void onError(Exception e) {
                future.completeExceptionally(e);
            }
        });
    });
    return future;
}
```

**Usage:**
```java
// Modern async API - thread-safe and easy to use
cluster.onAsync()
    .thenAccept(v -> log.info("Success"))
    .exceptionally(e -> { log.error("Failed", e); return null; });
```

**Pros:**
- Best developer experience
- Modern async/await pattern
- Thread-safe by design

**Cons:**
- More work to implement
- Requires code generator changes

## Recommended Solution

**Implement Option 1 immediately** (add StackLock to ChipInteractionClient methods) as it:
- Fixes the crash bug with minimal changes
- Makes Java SDK behavior consistent
- Requires no application code changes

**Then implement Option 3** (async API) for better developer experience in future releases.

## Environment

- **Matter SDK**: Latest main branch (Jan 2025)
- **Platform**: Linux ARM64, macOS ARM64
- **Language**: Java 21
- **Issue affects**: All Java applications using Matter SDK

## Steps to Reproduce

1. Create Java application using Matter SDK
2. Initialize Matter controller and commission a device
3. Call cluster command from ANY thread except Matter thread:

```java
new Thread(() -> {
    ChipClusters.OnOffCluster cluster =
        new ChipClusters.OnOffCluster(devicePtr, endpoint);
    cluster.on(new ChipClusters.DefaultClusterCallback() {
        @Override
        public void onSuccess() {
            System.out.println("Success");
        }

        @Override
        public void onError(Exception e) {
            System.err.println("Error: " + e);
        }
    });
}).start();
```

4. **Observe crash** with one of:
   - Segmentation fault (SIGSEGV)
   - Assertion failure: `assertChipStackLockedByCurrentThread()`
   - JVM crash

## Expected Behavior

Cluster commands should be thread-safe (like ChipDeviceController methods) or SDK should provide clear thread-safety utilities.

## Actual Behavior

- Cluster commands crash when called from non-Matter threads
- No documentation about thread requirements
- No built-in utilities for thread-safe execution
- Developers must implement custom JNI workarounds

## Additional Context

### Why This is Critical

1. **Every Java application is affected** - UI apps, server apps, all need workarounds
2. **Crashes are silent** - No compile-time warning, only runtime crashes
3. **Inconsistent with other methods** - ChipDeviceController has StackLock, cluster commands don't
4. **Python doesn't have this issue** - Makes Java SDK significantly inferior

### Files Involved

In Matter SDK repository:

- `src/controller/java/MatterInteractionClient-JNI.cpp` - Missing StackLock in invoke/read/subscribe
- `src/controller/java/generated/java/chip/devicecontroller/ChipClusters.java` - Generated unsafe methods
- `src/controller/java/CHIPDeviceController-JNI.cpp` - Has StackLock (good example)

### Related Issues

- Similar to iOS SDK dispatch queue requirements (but iOS provides utilities)
- Android CHIPTool example doesn't show this issue (all calls from UI thread)
- No documentation in `/docs/` about Java threading requirements

### Impact on Ecosystem

Without this fix:
- ❌ Java applications are unstable and crash-prone
- ❌ Developers waste time on workarounds instead of features
- ❌ Matter adoption in Java ecosystem is hindered
- ❌ Java SDK appears lower quality than Python SDK

With this fix:
- ✅ Java SDK matches Python SDK reliability
- ✅ Developers can focus on application logic
- ✅ Better Matter adoption in Android, server, and desktop Java apps
- ✅ Consistent and predictable SDK behavior

## Proposed Timeline

1. **Immediate (v1.4 or patch)**: Add StackLock to ChipInteractionClient JNI methods
2. **Next release (v1.5)**: Add executeOnChipThread() utility method
3. **Future (v2.0)**: Add async/Future-based API for modern Java patterns

---

**This bug makes Matter Java SDK nearly unusable in production applications.** A fix would significantly improve developer experience and application stability.

