body:
### Reproduction steps

All Kotlin clusters (generated code) that need to parse multiple TLV tags in one of their invoke functions won't be able to parse all of the elements due to a wrong "if" chaining.

All Kotlin clusters that must perform parse multiple elements from TLV are doing the same thing:

```kotlin
...
val tlvReader = TlvReader(response.payload)
...
// Define tags
val TAG_TOTO: Int = 0
val TAG_TATA: Int = 1
...
while (!tlvReader.isEndOfContainer()) {
      val tag = tlvReader.peekElement().tag

      if (tag == ContextSpecificTag(TAG_TOTO)) {
         // assigning data to a value here
      }

      if (tag == ContextSpecificTag(TAG_TATA)) {
        // assigning data to a value here
      } else {
        tlvReader.skipElement()
      }
}
...
```

The result is that every time that the tag is not the last one, a skipElement() is performed. This lead to many tags ignored at that step.

The fix is just to chain the "if" with "else" and then all elements are parsed successfully:

```kotlin
...
val tlvReader = TlvReader(response.payload)
...
// Define tags
val TAG_TOTO: Int = 0
val TAG_TATA: Int = 1
...
while (!tlvReader.isEndOfContainer()) {
      val tag = tlvReader.peekElement().tag

      if (tag == ContextSpecificTag(TAG_TOTO)) {
         // assigning data to a value here
      } else if (tag == ContextSpecificTag(TAG_TATA)) {
        // assigning data to a value here
      } else {
        tlvReader.skipElement()
      }
}
...
```

You can see example of the issue:
* https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/blob/master/src/controller/java/generated/java/matter/controller/cluster/clusters/OperationalCredentialsCluster.kt#L159
* https://github.com/project-chip/connectedhomeip/blob/master/src/controller/java/generated/java/matter/controller/cluster/clusters/ApplicationLauncherCluster.kt#L198

This seem to be a Kotlin code generator issue as all clusters that do the same thing are affected.


### Bug prevalence

All the time using the concerned clusters

### GitHub hash of the SDK that was being used

ce90b61

### Platform

android

### Platform Version(s)

_No response_

### Anything else?

_No response_

Matter / Project CHIP uses the `nlassert` third-party package in _src/lib/support/CodeUtils.h_ to implement basic, conditional runtime return status assertion handling through the use of the following interfaces in that package:

1. `NL_ASSERT_ABORT`
    * Allowed and expected `nlassert` customization implementing a Matter-specific override when configured `nlassert` behavior requests an abort condition.
2. `NL_ASSERT_LOG`
    * Allowed and expected `nlassert` customization implementing a Matter-specific override when configured `nlassert` behavior requests logging an assertion failure.
3. `nlEXPECT`
4. `nlEXPECT_ACTION`

(3) and (4) are then used in:

* _src/lib/support/CodeUtils.h_:`#define SuccessOrExit(error) nlEXPECT(::chip::ChipError::IsSuccess((error)), exit)`
* _src/lib/support/CodeUtils.h_:`#define SuccessOrExitAction(error, action) nlEXPECT_ACTION(::chip::ChipError::IsSuccess((error)), exit, action)`
* _src/lib/support/CodeUtils.h_:`#define VerifyOrExit(aCondition, anAction) nlEXPECT_ACTION(aCondition, exit, anAction)`
* _src/tools/chip-cert/chip-cert.h_:`#define VerifyTrueOrExit(aStatus) nlEXPECT(aStatus, exit)`
* _src/tracing/metric_macros.h_:`    nlEXPECT(::chip::Tracing::ErrorHandling::LogMetricIfError((kMetricKey), (error)), exit)`
* _src/tracing/metric_macros.h_:`    nlEXPECT_ACTION(aCondition, exit, MATTER_LOG_METRIC((kMetricKey), (anAction)))`

Where `SuccessOrExit`, `SuccessOrExitAction`, and `VerifyOrExit` are then used pervasively throughout the rest of the Matter code base to effect conditional runtime return status assertion handling.

All this is fine and good within the bounds of `nlassert` design and intent: customizable, conditional runtime return status assertion handling with a project's translation units (TUs). More succinctly, so long as the use is within a given C or C++ **source** file translation unit, the configuration for a project at large or for that specific TU does not "pollute" or impact another project or TU.

However, where this becomes problematic is when _CodeUtils.h_ and the chain of inline functions and macros it declares get included in other _public_ **header** files. These, in turn, get ingested in other TUs and other projects. The other TUs are fine insofar as they are within the Matter project. But, when the other projects are products or platforms that already use `nlassert` with **their own** custom configuration for that product and platform, the conflicts occur.

These conflicts can be mitigated with Matter forward declarations so long as Matter structures and classes are incorporated PIMPL-style and then moving up any source TU inclusion of the `nlassert.h` header (or headers that, in turn, include it such as `myassert.h` below) as:

Header:

```c++
#pragma once

#include <memory>

#include <stdint.h>

#include <mystatus.hpp>


// MARK: Forward Declarations

namespace chip
{

class ServerInitParams;

namespace Credentials
{

class DeviceAttestationCredentialsProvider;

}; // namespace Credentials

namespace DeviceLayer
{

class ChipDeviceEvent;
class CommissionableDataProvider;
class DeviceInfoProvider;
class DeviceInstanceInfoProvider;

}; // namespace DeviceLayer

}; // namespace chip

namespace my
{

namespace Matter
{

class Controller
{
public:
    // Con/destruction

                     Controller(void) noexcept;
                     ~Controller(void) noexcept = default;

    // Initialization

            status_t Init(void) noexcept;
            status_t Shutdown(void) noexcept;

    // Control

            status_t Run(void) noexcept;
            status_t Stop(void) noexcept;
            status_t Stop(const status_t &aStopStatus) noexcept;

    // Introspection

            bool     IsRunning(void) const noexcept;

private:
    // Event Handling

            void     EventHandler(const chip::DeviceLayer::ChipDeviceEvent & inEvent) noexcept;
    static  void     EventHandler(const chip::DeviceLayer::ChipDeviceEvent * inEvent,
                                  intptr_t inContext);

private:
    bool                                                           mRunning;
    status_t                                                       mStopStatus;
    std::unique_ptr<chip::DeviceLayer::CommissionableDataProvider> mCommissionableDataProvider;
    std::unique_ptr<chip::DeviceLayer::CommissionableDataProvider> mDeviceAttestationCredentialsProvider;
    std::unique_ptr<chip::DeviceLayer::DeviceInstanceInfoProvider> mDeviceInstanceInfoProvider;
    std::unique_ptr<chip::DeviceLayer::DeviceInfoProvider>         mDeviceInfoProvider;
    std::unique_ptr<chip::ServerInitParams>                        mServerInitParams;
};

}; // namespace Matter

}; // namespace my
```

Source:

```c++
#include "my-matter-controller.hpp"

#include <string>

// Normally, myassert.h would be grouped with other MY headers;
// however, due to both MY and Matter code using nlassert.h and
// defining both NL_ASSERT_USE_FLAGS_DEFAULT and NL_ASSERT_LOG,
// myassert.h must come first to avoid -Werror errors about
// redefining those two preprocessor symbols.

#include <myassert.h>

#include <Matter/App/Server/OnboardingCodesUtil.h>
#include <Matter/App/Server/Server.h>
#include <Matter/Core/CHIPError.h>
#include <Matter/Credentials/DeviceAttestationCredsProvider.h>
#include <Matter/Credentials/examples/DeviceAttestationCredsExample.h>
#include <Matter/Platform/CommissionableDataProvider.h>
#include <Matter/Platform/ConfigurationManager.h>
#include <Matter/Platform/ConnectivityManager.h>
#include <Matter/Platform/DeviceInfoProvider.h>
#include <Matter/Platform/DeviceInstanceInfoProvider.h>
#include <Matter/Platform/TestOnlyCommissionableDataProvider.h>
#include <Matter/Platform/PlatformManager.h>
#include <Matter/Setup/SetupPayload.h>
#include <Matter/Support/CHIPMem.h>
#include <Matter/Support/logging/CHIPLogging.h>

#include <myconditional.hpp>
#include <mylog.hpp>
#include <mytrace.h>

#include "my-matter-device-info-provider.hpp"


namespace my
{

namespace Matter
{

namespace Detail
{

static status_t
HandleError(
    const CHIP_ERROR &inError
) noexcept
{
    status_t lRetval = MY_ERROR_UNKNOWN;

    logEntry(LEVEL_ERROR,
             "Matter Controller: %s",
             inError.AsString());

    return (lRetval);
}

static log_level_t
MapLogCategory(
    const chip::Logging::LogCategory &inLogCategory
) noexcept
{
    log_level_t lRetval;

    switch (inLogCategory)
    {

    case chip::Logging::LogCategory::kLogCategory_Error:
        lRetval = LEVEL_ERROR;
        break;

    case chip::Logging::LogCategory::kLogCategory_Progress:
        lRetval = LEVEL_INFO;
        break;

    case chip::Logging::LogCategory::kLogCategory_Detail:
        lRetval = LEVEL_DEBUG;
        break;

    case chip::Logging::LogCategory::kLogCategory_Automation:
        lRetval = LEVEL_INFO;
        break;

    default:
        lRetval = LEVEL_INFO;
        break;

    }

    return (lRetval);
}

static void
MatterLogRedirectCallback(
    const char *inModule,
    uint8_t inCategory,
    const char *inMessage,
    va_list inArguments
) noexcept
{
    const chip::Logging::LogCategory lCategory = static_cast<chip::Logging::LogCategory>(inCategory);
    std::string                      lMessage;

    nlREQUIRE(inModule  != nullptr,                                      done);
    nlEXPECT(lCategory  != chip::Logging::LogCategory::kLogCategory_None, done);
    nlEXPECT(chip::Logging::IsCategoryEnabled(lCategory),                done);
    nlREQUIRE(inMessage != nullptr,                                      done);

    lMessage  = "Matter:";
    lMessage += inModule;
    lMessage += ": ";
    lMessage += inMessage;

    logEntry(MapLogCategory(lCategory), lMessage.c_str(), inArguments);

 done:
    return;
}

}; // namespace Detail

// MARK: Function and Method Implementation

// MARK: Station Manager Matter Controller

// MARK: Construction

Controller :: Controller(void) noexcept :
      mStopStatus(MY_STATUS_SUCCESS)
    , mCommissionableDataProvider()
    , mDeviceAttestationCredentialsProvider()
    , mDeviceInstanceInfoProvider()
    , mDeviceInfoProvider()
    , mServerInitParams()
{
    return;
}

// MARK: Initialization

status_t
Controller :: Init(void) noexcept
{
    // The controller can be provisioned either over BLE or on-network.

    static constexpr chip::RendezvousInformationFlags kRendezvousInformationFlags = {
        chip::RendezvousInformationFlag::kBLE,
        chip::RendezvousInformationFlag::kOnNetwork
    };

    CHIP_ERROR lStatus;
    status_t   lRetval = MY_STATUS_SUCCESS;

    // Set the logging redirect callback such that we can redirect
    // from the default C Standard Output output to the current
    // process log stream.

    chip::Logging::SetLogRedirectCallback(Detail::MatterLogRedirectCallback);

    // Initialize the Matter dynamic memory allocation subsystem.

    lStatus = chip::Platform::MemoryInit();
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    // Initialize the Matter device layer platform manager.

    lStatus = chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().InitChipStack();
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    // Set the provider for commissionable data (that is, a set of
    // data for commissioning that includes the setup discriminiator;
    // SPAKE2+ iteration count, salt, and verifier; and setup code).
    //
    // For now, allocate and use a test-only instance.

    mCommissionableDataProvider.reset(new (std::nothrow) chip::DeviceLayer::TestOnlyCommissionableDataProvider);
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(mCommissionableDataProvider != nullptr, done, lRetval = -EINVAL);

    chip::DeviceLayer::SetCommissionableDataProvider(mCommissionableDataProvider.get());

    // Set the provider for the device (class) information (that is,
    // fixed labels, user labels, supported locales, and supported
    // calendar types).

    mDeviceInfoProvider.reset(new (std::nothrow) DeviceInfoProvider);
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(mDeviceInfoProvider != nullptr, done, lRetval = -EINVAL);

    chip::DeviceLayer::SetDeviceInfoProvider(mDeviceInfoProvider.get());

    // Set the provider for the Device Attestation Credentials (that
    // is, certification declaration, firmware information, device
    // attestation certificate (DAC), product attestation intermediate
    // (PAI) certificate, and device attestation signing).
    //
    // For now, just use the Matter credentials example provider.

    chip::Credentials::SetDeviceAttestationCredentialsProvider(chip::Credentials::Examples::GetExampleDACProvider());

    // Initialize the Matter data model and app cluster server.
    //
    // For the purposes of prototyping, leverage an instance of the
    // transitional chip::CommonCaseDeviceServerInitParams.

    mServerInitParams.reset(new (std::nothrow) chip::CommonCaseDeviceServerInitParams);
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(mServerInitParams != nullptr, done, lRetval = -EINVAL);

    lStatus = std::static_pointer_cast<chip::CommonCaseDeviceServerInitParams>(mServerInitParams)->InitializeStaticResourcesBeforeServerInit();
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    lStatus = chip::Server::GetInstance().Init(*mServerInitParams.get());
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    // Log the device configuration.

    chip::DeviceLayer::ConfigurationMgr().LogDeviceConfig();

    // Log the provisioning QRCode URL and short, manual pairing code,
    // based on the allowed rendezvous information flags.
    //
    // This uses the commissionable data provider set above to pull
    // the relevant commissionable data necessary to build the QRCode
    // and get the short, manual pairing code.

    PrintOnboardingCodes(kRendezvousInformationFlags);

    // Add our platform manager event handler.

    lStatus = chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().AddEventHandler(EventHandler, reinterpret_cast<intptr_t>(this));
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    // Set BLE device advertising name to the default
    // "MATTER-<discriminator>".
    //
    // For production, we may want this to be "MY-<discriminator>".

    lStatus = chip::DeviceLayer::ConnectivityMgr().SetBLEDeviceName(nullptr);
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    // Enable BLE advertising.

    lStatus = chip::DeviceLayer::ConnectivityMgr().SetBLEAdvertisingEnabled(true);
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    // At this point, initialization is complete. From here, the
    // Matter event / run loop needs to be started. This can be done
    // with either 'chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().RunEventLoop()'
    // or 'chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().StartEventLoopTask()'. The
    // former is for applications that want to manage their own thread
    // context and run the Matter stack on it whereas the latter is
    // for applications that want Matter to manage an internal thread
    // context and run the Matter stack on that. The latter uses a
    // non-detached, joinable thread.
    //
    // That action will be handled in the 'Run' method.

    lRetval = MY_STATUS_SUCCESS;

 done:
    return (lRetval);
}

status_t
Controller :: Shutdown(void) noexcept
{
    CHIP_ERROR lStatus;
    status_t   lRetval = MY_STATUS_SUCCESS;

    // Disable BLE advertising.

    lStatus = chip::DeviceLayer::ConnectivityMgr().SetBLEAdvertisingEnabled(false);
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

    // Remove our platform manager event handler.

    chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().RemoveEventHandler(EventHandler, reinterpret_cast<intptr_t>(this));

    // Shutdown the Matter data model and app cluster server.

    chip::Server::GetInstance().Shutdown();

    // Shutdown the Matter device layer platform manager.

    chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().Shutdown();

    // Shutdown the Matter dynamic memory allocation subsystem.

    chip::Platform::MemoryShutdown();

    // Reset the logging redirect callback.

    chip::Logging::SetLogRedirectCallback(nullptr);

 done:
    return (lRetval);
}

// MARK: Control

status_t
Controller :: Run(void) noexcept
{
    CHIP_ERROR lStatus;
    status_t   lRetval = MY_STATUS_SUCCESS;

    // Attempt to start the Matter thread event / run loop.

    lStatus = chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().StartEventLoopTask();
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

 done:
    return (lRetval);
}

status_t
Controller :: Stop(void) noexcept
{
    return (Stop(MY_STATUS_SUCCESS));
}

status_t
Controller :: Stop(
    const status_t &aStopStatus
) noexcept
{
    CHIP_ERROR lStatus;
    status_t   lRetval = MY_STATUS_SUCCESS;

    nlEXPECT_ACTION(IsRunning(), done, lRetval = -EALREADY);

    // Save the specified stop status for later observation.

    lRetval = Conditional::Set(aStopStatus, mStopStatus);
    nlREQUIRE(lRetval >= MY_STATUS_SUCCESS, done);

    // Attempt to stop the Matter thread event / run loop.

    lStatus = chip::DeviceLayer::PlatformMgr().StopEventLoopTask();
    nlREQUIRE_ACTION(lStatus == CHIP_NO_ERROR, done, lRetval = Detail::HandleError(lStatus));

 done:
    return (lRetval);
}

// MARK: Event Handling

void
Controller :: EventHandler(
    const chip::DeviceLayer::ChipDeviceEvent & inEvent
) noexcept
{
    logEntry(LEVEL_DEBUG,
             "Matter Controller: "
             "received %splatform-%s event %hu",
             (inEvent.IsPublic() ?
              "public " :
              (inEvent.IsInternal() ? "internal " : "unknown ")),
             (inEvent.IsPlatformSpecific() ?
              "specific" :
              (inEvent.IsPlatformGeneric() ? "generic" : "unknown")),
             inEvent.Type);

    switch (inEvent.Type)
    {

    default:
        break;

    }
}

void
Controller :: EventHandler(
    const chip::DeviceLayer::ChipDeviceEvent * inEvent,
    intptr_t inContext
)
{
    Controller * const lController = reinterpret_cast<Controller *>(inContext);

    nlREQUIRE(lController != nullptr, done);
    nlREQUIRE(inEvent     != nullptr, done);

    lController->EventHandler(*inEvent);

 done:
    return;
}

}; // namespace Matter

}; // namespace my
```

However, when it comes to things like descending from a provider interface, that work around has to be pushed further against product or project style, since the `nlassert`-_tainted_ Matter header is already pulled in:

Header:

```c++
