body:
### Reproduction steps

Zephyr/nrfconnect Matter device (observed on nRF54LM20 + OTBR; the code path is platform-generic Zephyr), commissioned into a Thread network as a child:

1. Commission the device. Reboot it once while attached (any attached reboot re-announces a frame counter advanced by `OPENTHREAD_CONFIG_STORE_FRAME_COUNTER_AHEAD`, typically 100000).
2. Factory-reset the device (`CONFIG_CHIP_FACTORY_RESET_ERASE_SETTINGS=y` path).
3. Immediately re-commission it (chip-tool `pairing code-thread`).

Result: commissioning times out at the Thread attach stage (`CHIP Error 0x00000032: Timeout`). The border router's own `child table` still carries the pre-reset entry for the device's extended address through the failure. Waiting out the child timeout (240 s default) before retrying makes it pass — exactly the window a user re-adding their device does not wait.

This is aggravated on devices whose extended address is stable across the reset (vendor-pinned identity, as in Nordic's known issue KRKNWK-18916): OpenThread's counter persistence explicitly assumes a factory reset "also starts with a new randomly generated extended address" (comment in `openthread/src/core/thread/mle.cpp`), and this reset path violates the counterpart assumption that the parent is told anything at all.

### Mechanism

`DoFactoryReset` (`src/platform/Zephyr/ConfigurationManagerImpl.cpp` @ 7d6d9aff4ded) is completely silent on-air in the `CONFIG_CHIP_FACTORY_RESET_ERASE_SETTINGS` branch:

```cpp
#if CHIP_DEVICE_CONFIG_ENABLE_THREAD_SRP_CLIENT
    TEMPORARY_RETURN_IGNORED ThreadStackMgr().ClearAllSrpHostAndServices();
#endif // CHIP_DEVICE_CONFIG_ENABLE_THREAD_SRP_CLIENT

// Lock the Thread stack to avoid unwanted interaction with settings NVS during factory reset.
#if defined(CONFIG_OPENTHREAD) || defined(CONFIG_NET_L2_OPENTHREAD)
    ThreadStackMgr().LockThreadStack();
#endif
    // ...settings wipe...
    PlatformMgr().Shutdown();   // sys_reboot(SYS_REBOOT_WARM)
```

- No `otThreadSetEnabled(false)` and no `otThreadDetachGracefully` on this path — the only Thread disable lives in `_ErasePersistentInfo()`, reached solely via the `#else` (non-ERASE_SETTINGS) branch. `otThreadDetachGracefully` has no caller anywhere on the factory-reset path.
- The parent router therefore keeps the child-table entry, and with it the per-ext-addr MLE frame-counter replay floor (`VerifyOrExit(frameCounter >= neighbor->GetMleFrameCounter(), error = kErrorDuplicated)` — no attach-message exemption). The reborn device presents counter ~0 under the same address and every MLE attach message is silently dropped until child-timeout eviction.
- Note the `LockThreadStack()` above is never released — the function ends in `sys_reboot()` — so any fix must complete its on-air work before that lock.

Two adjacent SRP defects compound it (the retry-by-reset a user attempts after the failed attach hits them):

- The SRP clear-all is unverified best-effort: the result is discarded (now explicitly `TEMPORARY_RETURN_IGNORED`), and Zephyr's `_WaitOnSrpClearAllComplete()` drops the `k_sem_take` return code, so an unconfirmed removal (device detached, server unreachable) is silent. The wipe then destroys the SRP client's ECDSA key, and the server's first-come-first-served name defense refuses any later update to those names ("duplicated") for up to the granted key lease. #21101 acknowledges the same stranding on the dataset-revert path.
- `_ClearAllSrpHostAndServices()` waits on the confirmation semaphore even when the `otSrpClientRemoveHostAndServices` submission itself failed (e.g. `OT_ERROR_ALREADY`, host already removed) — the callback can never fire, so the reset burns the full 2 s timeout for nothing.

### Suggested fix (validated downstream)

Insert a bounded graceful detach between the SRP clear-all and `LockThreadStack()`:

```cpp
#ifdef CONFIG_OPENTHREAD
    {
        otInstance * otInst = openthread_get_default_instance();

        if (otInst != nullptr)
        {
            otError detachError = OT_ERROR_INVALID_STATE;

            ThreadStackMgr().LockThreadStack();
            const otDeviceRole role = otThreadGetDeviceRole(otInst);
            if (role != OT_DEVICE_ROLE_DISABLED && role != OT_DEVICE_ROLE_DETACHED)
            {
                detachError = otThreadDetachGracefully(otInst, DetachGracefullyDone, nullptr);
            }
            ThreadStackMgr().UnlockThreadStack();

            if (detachError == OT_ERROR_NONE && k_sem_take(&sDetachGracefullySem, K_SECONDS(2)) != 0)
            {
                ChipLogError(DeviceLayer, "Graceful Thread detach timed out; the parent may keep this node's entry");
            }
        }
    }
#endif // CONFIG_OPENTHREAD
```

with a file-static `K_SEM_DEFINE(sDetachGracefullySem, 0, 1)` and a callback that does nothing but `k_sem_give` (it can be invoked synchronously from inside `otThreadDetachGracefully()` while the Thread stack lock is held). Ordering matters and was proven empirically: the SRP removal is only deliverable while attached, so it must run first; the detach's Timeout=0 Child Update then makes the parent delete the entry (and the counter floor) immediately. The wait is bounded because this runs on the Matter event loop with the stack lock held, and the reset proceeds regardless of the outcome.

Downstream validation (nRF54LM20 + OTBR, NCS v3.3.x vintage of these files, patch applies to master with minor context drift): without the detach, the repro above fails deterministically (~31 s, `0x32`) with the BR child entry surviving the wipe; with it, the entry vanishes sub-second (0.3–0.7 s across runs) and the immediate re-commission completes in ~13 s — measured both alongside an app-level workaround and with the patch standing alone (3/3 passes each). The two SRP repairs (log the unconfirmed clear-all; skip the wait when the submission fails) were validated in the same runs.

### Related

- #73163 / #73600 — the same missing-graceful-detach mechanism on the network-*switching* path; #73600 patches only the attach path, not the factory-reset path.
- #33501 — introduced the (best-effort) SRP clear-all on this path.
- #21101 — stale SRP registrations acknowledged on the dataset-revert path.
- #16970 — this exact symptom (BR expects counter 744043530, reset device sends 0, commissioning error 0x32), closed by the stale bot in 2022 without a root cause.
- #32900 — stale SRP/DNS-SD records after reset picked up by the controller.
- Nordic KRKNWK-18916 — downstream workaround that regenerates the extended address because the SRP server still holds the old one (the assumption this path leans on).

## Summary

`TC_MOD_1_2.py` step 5 asserts `isinstance(startup_mode, int)`, which fails when `StartUpMode` is null. Per the Matter spec, null is a valid value for `StartUpMode` (meaning no startup override), but the test does not handle this case.

## Workaround in place

`ModeSelectCluster::ApplyStartupModeLogic()` in `src/app/clusters/mode-select-server/ModeSelectCluster.cpp` contains the following workaround:

```cpp
// TODO: remove this workaround once TC_MOD_1_2.py is updated to accept a null StartUpMode.
// StartUpMode is nullable per spec (null = no startup override), but TC_MOD_1_2 step 5 asserts
// isinstance(startup_mode, int). Until that test is fixed, default to CurrentMode on first boot.
if (mOptionalAttributeSet.IsSet(StartUpMode::Id) && mStartUpMode.IsNull())
{
    mStartUpMode.SetNonNull(mCurrentMode);
}
```

This initializes `StartUpMode` to `CurrentMode` on first boot when `StartUpMode` is null and not yet persisted, so the test assertion passes.

## What needs to be done

- Identify exactly which step in `TC_MOD_1_2.py` performs `isinstance(startup_mode, int)`.
- Update the test to accept a null `StartUpMode` value as a valid response.
- Once the test is fixed, remove the workaround from `ApplyStartupModeLogic()` and restore the spec-compliant behavior (null `StartUpMode` = no startup override).

## Reference

Added in PR #72957 (Mode Select code-driven migration).

### Summary

On ESP32 with NimBLE, `BLEManagerImpl::HandleGAPPeripheralConnect` does not check `gapEvent->connect.status`. NimBLE's `ble_gap.h` documents that field as 0 when "the connection was successfully established", and otherwise as a host error code meaning "the connection attempt failed".

One such failure strands the device. For a peripheral, NimBLE posts the successful `BLE_GAP_EVENT_CONNECT` only after it has read the central's version and features. If the link drops before then, `ble_gap_conn_broken()` in `nimble/host/src/ble_gap.c` finds that no connect event was ever posted for the connection. It then posts `BLE_GAP_EVENT_CONNECT` with status `BLE_HS_EAGAIN` (1) *instead of* `BLE_GAP_EVENT_DISCONNECT`, and its comment says this is so that applications can restart advertising.

CHIP instead logs "BLE GAP connection established", increments `mNumGAPCons` and takes a slot in `mSubscribedConIds`. Only a disconnect event frees that slot, and for this connection none will ever arrive.

With `CONFIG_CHIPOBLE_SINGLE_CONNECTION=y`, the Kconfig default, `DriveBLEState` advertises only while `_NumConnections() == 0`. So CHIPoBLE advertising stops and does not restart: we watched for 50 s in the reproduction below and over five minutes in the field. A device in its commissioning window disappears from every commissioner, and nothing short of a reboot frees the slot. We did not test `=n`, but from the code `StartAdvertising` would then advertise non-connectable, because `kMaxConnections` is `BLE_LAYER_NUM_BLE_ENDPOINTS`, which defaults to 1.

Any central can trigger it by cancelling a connection just as the link comes up, while NimBLE is still reading the peer's version and features. We first hit it with a phone app whose bug cancelled connections almost immediately. Commissioner apps that give up on a slow connection could plausibly hit it too.

The central-role handler, `HandleGAPCentralConnect`, already checks the status. The peripheral handler is unchanged on `master` as of 0d7f692da0.

### Reproduction steps

Setup: ESP32-C3, esp-matter `release/v1.6` (31b76ad145) with connectedhomeip 93abd8e689, ESP-IDF v5.5.5. It reproduces on esp-matter's unmodified `examples/light`, built for esp32c3 with its default configuration (`CONFIG_CHIPOBLE_SINGLE_CONNECTION=y`, `CONFIG_BT_NIMBLE_MAX_CONNECTIONS=3`), uncommissioned, with test discriminator 3840.

We first found it on our own light, which is that example plus one extra GATT service with a GAP event listener that only logs. The longer logs below come from that build, because its listener prints the connect status.

1. Open the commissioning window on the device.
2. On a Mac, build and run the script below from Terminal. We used a Mac17,2 on macOS 26.6.2, with the built-in Broadcom BCM4388C2 Bluetooth controller. `swiftc` comes with Xcode or the Command Line Tools, and macOS asks for Terminal's Bluetooth permission on the first run. It repeatedly connects to the device, cancels 0-120 ms later, and waits for the device to advertise again:
   ```
   swiftc -O repro-cancel-during-connect.swift -o repro
   ./repro --discriminator <device discriminator>
   ```
3. When a cancel lands at the wrong moment in connection setup, NimBLE logs `hci_err=0x202 : BLE_ERR_UNK_CONN_ID` for LE Read Remote Features. CHIP then logs "BLE GAP connection established" and "CHIPoBLE advertising stopped", nothing follows, and the script exits 2 after 30 s with no advertisement. A GAP event listener shows the connect event arriving with status 1 and no disconnect after it.

**Stock example, without the fix:** the script reproduced it on attempt 2. The device log:

```
I (1482) chip[DL]: CHIPoBLE advertising started
I (13972) chip[DL]: BLE GAP connection terminated (con 1 reason 0x213)
I (13982) chip[DL]: Configuring CHIPoBLE advertising (interval 25 ms, connectable)
I (15202) NimBLE: ogf=0x08, ocf=0x0016, hci_err=0x202 : BLE_ERR_UNK_CONN_ID (Unknown Connection Identifier)
I (15212) chip[DL]: BLE GAP connection established (con 1)
I (15222) chip[DL]: CHIPoBLE advertising stopped
```

The device logged nothing further, and the script exited 2 after 30 s with no advertisement.

**Stock example, with the fix:** all 300 attempts completed, the fix fired 113 times, and the device never stopped advertising.

**Our light, without the fix:** the script reproduced it on attempt 23, 27 s into the run:

```
17:42:38.015 found <peripheral> at -41 dBm; 300 attempts, each cancelled 0-120 ms after connect()
...
17:43:04.889 attempt 23: cancelled 45 ms after connect(); link up first: no
17:43:21.687   no advertisement for 15 s; restarting the scan before blaming the device
17:43:37.434 REPRODUCED on attempt 23: no advertisement for 30 s after the cancel
```

The device log for the previous attempt and for attempt 23, filtered to connection and advertising lines. `MCBNimble` is the extra service's GAP listener, which only logs. The device logged nothing more, of any kind, in the 50 s before it was reflashed:

```
I (41024) MCBNimble: [GAP] CONNECT: conn=1 status=0 t=41024ms
I (41024) chip[DL]: BLE GAP connection established (con 1)
I (41034) chip[DL]: CHIPoBLE advertising stopped
I (41054) MCBNimble: [GAP] DISCONNECT: conn=1 reason=0x213 t=41054ms (conn_dur=30ms)
I (41064) chip[DL]: BLE GAP connection terminated (con 1 reason 0x213)
I (41084) chip[DL]: Configuring CHIPoBLE advertising (interval 25 ms, connectable)
I (41114) chip[DL]: CHIPoBLE advertising started
I (42164) NimBLE: ogf=0x08, ocf=0x0016, hci_err=0x202 : BLE_ERR_UNK_CONN_ID (Unknown Connection Identifier)
I (42174) MCBNimble: [GAP] CONNECT: conn=1 status=1 t=42174ms
I (42184) chip[DL]: BLE GAP connection established (con 1)
I (42184) chip[DL]: CHIPoBLE advertising stopped
```

That run produced 19 connect events: 11 with status 0, 7 with status 19 and 1 with status 1. The status-19 events come from NimBLE's LE Read Remote Features Complete handler, which passes through the controller's HCI status (0x13, remote user terminated) and marks the connection as reported. Note that 19 is numerically also `BLE_HS_ETIMEOUT_HCI`. It is the raw HCI code here: the disconnect that follows carries reason 0x213, which is `BLE_HS_ERR_HCI_BASE` + 0x13. So `BLE_GAP_EVENT_DISCONNECT` did follow each one, and CHIP recovered every time. The status-1 event came right after the LE Read Remote Features command itself failed with `BLE_ERR_UNK_CONN_ID`. That is the `ble_gap_conn_broken()` path described above: no disconnect followed, and advertising never resumed.

<details>
<summary>repro-cancel-during-connect.swift</summary>

```swift
// repro-cancel-during-connect.swift
//
// Reproduces a CHIPoBLE advertising stall on ESP32 with NimBLE. A BLE central
// that cancels a connection while an ESP32 is still setting it up leaves CHIP counting a
// connection that never existed, and CHIPoBLE advertising never restarts.
//
// It finds one commissionable Matter device by its discriminator. Then, over and over, it connects
// and cancels after a random delay, and waits for the device to advertise again. If the device
// stays silent, it has gone dark and the script exits 2.
//
// Build it, then run it from Terminal.app. macOS asks Terminal for Bluetooth permission the first
// time:
//   swiftc -O repro-cancel-during-connect.swift -o repro
//   ./repro --discriminator <discriminator>
//
// On the device, a hit is a GAP connect event with a non-zero status that no disconnect follows.
// Without the fix, CHIP logs "BLE GAP connection established" and "CHIPoBLE advertising stopped"
// for it, and nothing after that. With the proposed fix, it logs "not counted, advertising resumes".
// The commissioning window has to stay open for the whole run, or advertising stops for an
// unrelated reason.
//
// Exit codes: 0 attempts finished and the device kept advertising, 1 setup problem,
// 2 reproduced.

import CoreBluetooth
import Foundation

let matterService = CBUUID(string: "FFF6")

struct Options {
    var discriminator: UInt16 = 0
    var attempts = 300
    var minDelayMs = 0
    var maxDelayMs = 120
    var silence = 15.0
}

func fail(_ message: String) -> Never {
    FileHandle.standardError.write((message + "\n").data(using: .utf8)!)
    exit(1)
}

func parseOptions() -> Options {
    let usage = "usage: repro --discriminator N [--attempts 300] [--min-ms 0] [--max-ms 120] [--silence 15]"
    var o = Options()
    var sawDiscriminator = false
    var args = CommandLine.arguments.dropFirst().makeIterator()
    while let a = args.next() {
        guard let v = args.next() else { fail(usage) }
        switch a {
        case "--discriminator": o.discriminator = UInt16(v) ?? 0; sawDiscriminator = UInt16(v) != nil
        case "--attempts": o.attempts = Int(v) ?? o.attempts
        case "--min-ms": o.minDelayMs = Int(v) ?? o.minDelayMs
        case "--max-ms": o.maxDelayMs = Int(v) ?? o.maxDelayMs
        case "--silence": o.silence = Double(v) ?? o.silence
        default: fail(usage)
        }
    }
    guard sawDiscriminator, o.discriminator <= 0x0FFF, o.minDelayMs >= 0, o.minDelayMs <= o.maxDelayMs else { fail(usage) }
    return o
}

final class Repro: NSObject, CBCentralManagerDelegate {
    enum Phase { case searching, connecting, settling, waitingForAdvert }

    let o: Options
    var central: CBCentralManager!
    var target: CBPeripheral?
    var phase = Phase.searching
    var attempt = 0
    var linkUpCount = 0
    var connectCalled = Date()
    var cancelled = Date()
    var linkUp: Date?
    // Bumped on every attempt so timers left over from an earlier attempt do nothing.
    var token = 0

    init(_ o: Options) {
        self.o = o
        super.init()
        central = CBCentralManager(delegate: self, queue: .main)
    }

    func log(_ s: String) {
        let t = ISO8601DateFormatter.string(from: Date(), timeZone: .current,
                                            formatOptions: [.withTime, .withColonSeparatorInTime, .withFractionalSeconds])
        print("\(t) \(s)")
        fflush(stdout)
    }

    func startScan() {
        // Duplicates on: silence has to mean the device stopped, not that the scan stopped reporting it.
        central.scanForPeripherals(withServices: [matterService],
                                   options: [CBCentralManagerScanOptionAllowDuplicatesKey: true])
    }

    func centralManagerDidUpdateState(_ c: CBCentralManager) {
        switch c.state {
        case .poweredOn:
            log("scanning for a commissionable Matter device with discriminator \(o.discriminator)")
            startScan()
            DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 30) { [self] in
                if phase == .searching { fail("no device advertised discriminator \(o.discriminator) within 30 s") }
            }
        case .unauthorized:
            fail("Bluetooth permission denied. Allow it for this terminal app in System Settings > Privacy & Security > Bluetooth.")
        case .poweredOff, .unsupported:
            fail("Bluetooth is off or unsupported (state \(c.state.rawValue))")
        default:
            break
        }
    }

    func centralManager(_ c: CBCentralManager, didDiscover p: CBPeripheral,
                        advertisementData: [String: Any], rssi: NSNumber) {
        // Matter core spec 5.4.2.5.6: opcode, then the 12-bit discriminator in the low bits of a
        // little-endian uint16 whose top 4 bits are the advertisement version.
        guard let serviceData = advertisementData[CBAdvertisementDataServiceDataKey] as? [CBUUID: Data],
              let d = serviceData[matterService], d.count >= 3 else { return }
        let b = [UInt8](d)
        let discriminator = (UInt16(b[1]) | UInt16(b[2]) << 8) & 0x0FFF
        guard b[0] == 0x00, discriminator == o.discriminator else { return }

        switch phase {
        case .searching:
            target = p
            log("found \(p.identifier) at \(rssi) dBm; \(o.attempts) attempts, each cancelled \(o.minDelayMs)-\(o.maxDelayMs) ms after connect()")
            nextAttempt()
        case .waitingForAdvert where p.identifier == target?.identifier:
            log(String(format: "  advertising again %.2f s after the cancel", Date().timeIntervalSince(cancelled)))
            nextAttempt()
        default:
            break
        }
    }

    func centralManager(_ c: CBCentralManager, didConnect p: CBPeripheral) {
        if phase == .connecting, p.identifier == target?.identifier { linkUp = Date() }
    }

    func nextAttempt() {
        guard let p = target else { return }
        guard attempt < o.attempts else {
            log("done: \(attempt) attempts, link up before the cancel in \(linkUpCount); the device kept advertising, not reproduced")
            exit(0)
        }
        attempt += 1
        token += 1
        let t = token
        linkUp = nil
        phase = .connecting
        let delay = Int.random(in: o.minDelayMs...o.maxDelayMs)
        connectCalled = Date()
        central.connect(p, options: nil)

        DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + .milliseconds(delay)) { [self] in
            guard t == token else { return }
            central.cancelPeripheralConnection(p)
            cancelled = Date()
            var up = "no"
            if let linkUp {
                linkUpCount += 1
                up = String(format: "yes, %.0f ms after connect()", linkUp.timeIntervalSince(connectCalled) * 1000)
            }
            log("attempt \(attempt): cancelled \(delay) ms after connect(); link up first: \(up)")
            // Let the disconnect finish and any reports already in flight drain before listening.
            phase = .settling
            DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + 1.0) { [self] in
                guard t == token else { return }
                waitForAdvert(t)
            }
        }
    }

    func waitForAdvert(_ t: Int) {
        phase = .waitingForAdvert
        DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + o.silence) { [self] in
            guard t == token, phase == .waitingForAdvert else { return }
            log("  no advertisement for \(Int(o.silence)) s; restarting the scan before blaming the device")
            central.stopScan()
            startScan()
            DispatchQueue.main.asyncAfter(deadline: .now() + o.silence) { [self] in
                guard t == token, phase == .waitingForAdvert else { return }
                log("REPRODUCED on attempt \(attempt): no advertisement for \(Int(2 * o.silence)) s after the cancel")
                exit(2)
            }
        }
    }
}

let repro = Repro(parseOptions())
RunLoop.main.run()
```

</details>

### Proposed fix

Don't count a connection whose status is non-zero, and ask for an advertising refresh. `ble_svr_gap_event` already schedules `DriveBLEState` after every event, and `StartAdvertising` already stops and restarts an advertisement that is still active.

```diff
diff --git a/src/platform/ESP32/nimble/BLEManagerImpl.cpp b/src/platform/ESP32/nimble/BLEManagerImpl.cpp
index 71463d6..6837889 100644
--- a/src/platform/ESP32/nimble/BLEManagerImpl.cpp
+++ b/src/platform/ESP32/nimble/BLEManagerImpl.cpp
@@ -1681,6 +1681,19 @@ CHIP_ERROR BLEManagerImpl::HandleGAPCentralConnect(struct ble_gap_event * gapEve
 CHIP_ERROR BLEManagerImpl::HandleGAPPeripheralConnect(struct ble_gap_event * gapEvent)
 {
     CHIP_ERROR err = CHIP_NO_ERROR;
+
+    // A non-zero status means the connection was never established. NimBLE reports a link that
+    // drops during connection setup as BLE_GAP_EVENT_CONNECT with an error and sends no
+    // BLE_GAP_EVENT_DISCONNECT for it, so counting it would hold a connection slot forever and,
+    // with CHIPOBLE_SINGLE_CONNECTION, keep CHIPoBLE advertising stopped until reboot.
+    if (gapEvent->connect.status != 0)
+    {
+        ChipLogError(DeviceLayer, "BLE GAP connection failed during setup (con %u status %d); not counted, advertising resumes",
+                     gapEvent->connect.conn_handle, gapEvent->connect.status);
+        mFlags.Set(Flags::kAdvertisingRefreshNeeded);
+        return CHIP_NO_ERROR;
+    }
+
     ChipLogProgress(DeviceLayer, "BLE GAP connection established (con %u)", gapEvent->connect.conn_handle);

     // Track the number of active GAP connections.
```

**Our light, with the fix:** the same script ran all 300 attempts and the device never stopped advertising. The script exited `0`.

The device logged 233 connect events, with statuses 0: 110, 1: 29, 19: 94. The fix fired on all 123 with a non-zero status, including 29 with status 1. After every one, CHIP reconfigured advertising within 20 ms. 94 of the 123 were followed by a disconnect event and 29 were not. Those 29 would each have stranded the device without the fix. Mac-side, the median time from cancel to the next advertisement was 1.19 s.

The first status-1 event with the fix:

```
I (19938) chip[DL]: CHIPoBLE advertising started
I (22048) NimBLE: ogf=0x08, ocf=0x0016, hci_err=0x202 : BLE_ERR_UNK_CONN_ID (Unknown Connection Identifier)
I (22048) MCBNimble: [GAP] CONNECT: conn=1 status=1 t=22048ms
E (22058) chip[DL]: BLE GAP connection failed during setup (con 1 status 1); not counted, advertising resumes
I (22068) chip[DL]: Configuring CHIPoBLE advertising (interval 25 ms, connectable)
I (23248) MCBNimble: [GAP] CONNECT: conn=1 status=0 t=23248ms
```

Why the change is safe when a disconnect does follow: NimBLE sends `BLE_GAP_EVENT_DISCONNECT` after some failed connects, such as status 19. With the fix, `HandleGAPDisconnect` then runs for a connection that was never counted. `mNumGAPCons` is decremented only under its `> 0` guard, and that guard is the only place it is read. `UnsetSubscribed` returns false, so no `kCHIPoBLEConnectionError` is posted. After the disconnect, CHIP's state is the same as without the fix.

Scope: only the NimBLE variant was tested. In `src/platform/ESP32/bluedroid/BLEManagerImpl.cpp`, `ESP_GATTS_CONNECT_EVT` carries no status and arrives only for connections that succeed, so the same flaw does not apply there as written.

I can open this as a PR if that is preferred.

### Bug prevalence

Whenever a central cancels a connection at the wrong moment in link setup. With the script above, 30 of 252 connect events across the two runs had status 1. Without the fix it reproduced on attempt 23; with the fix, status 1 occurred 29 times in 300 attempts.

