`ModeBaseCluster` answers an unsupported `ChangeToMode` / `ChangeToModeByCoreTag` request with `UnsupportedMode` and no `StatusText` field. Every derived mode cluster (Dishwasher, Laundry Washer, Refrigerator/TCC, Oven, RVC Run/Clean, DEM, EVSE, Water Heater, Thermostat Mode) uses this code. Goal: the response matches the spec.

**`StatusText` is absent on the wire for `UnsupportedMode`, but the spec makes it mandatory for every status other than Success.**
Why: a client or test that reads the response by the spec conformance (`[Status == Success], M`) sees a missing mandatory field. No certification test checks `StatusText` for `UnsupportedMode` today, so this passes certification.
Code: `src/app/clusters/mode-base-server/ModeBaseCluster.cpp:372-383` (ChangeToMode), `:422-432` (ChangeToModeByCoreTag)
```cpp
// We are leaving the StatusText empty since the Status is descriptive enough.
if (!IsSupportedMode(newMode))
{
    response.status = to_underlying(StatusCode::kUnsupportedMode);
    commandObj.AddResponse(commandPath, response);
```
`response.statusText` is `Optional<chip::CharSpan>` and is never set, so the field is left out instead of being sent empty.
Spec: Mode Base, Status Field: "If the Status field is set to UnsupportedMode, the StatusText field SHALL be an empty string." Response table: StatusText `[Status == Success], M`.
Fix: `response.statusText.SetValue(""_span);` in both places, and update the code comment, which quotes the "MAY ... human readable string" wording.

Related spec PR: https://github.com/CHIP-Specifications/connectedhomeip-spec/pull/13677 removes the conflicting "MAY ... human readable" wording, so the spec says "empty string" everywhere.

The code-driven Operational State cluster reads `PhaseList` and `OperationalStateList` from the delegate on each read, and stores `CurrentPhase` and `OperationalState` in the cluster. The setters check new values against the lists. The list-change calls do not check the stored values again. The goal is that `CurrentPhase` and `OperationalState` stay valid for the current lists.

**After a list change, CurrentPhase and OperationalState keep their old values, also when the values are no longer valid.**
Why: a controller that uses `CurrentPhase` as an index into `PhaseList` can read an index past the end of the list, or a non-null `CurrentPhase` while `PhaseList` is null. A controller can also read an `OperationalState` ID that is not in `OperationalStateList`.
Code: `OperationalStateCluster.cpp:140-149`:
```cpp
void OperationalStateCluster::ReportOperationalStateListChange()
{
    NotifyAttributeChanged(OperationalState::Attributes::OperationalStateList::Id);
}
void OperationalStateCluster::ReportPhaseListChange()
{
    NotifyAttributeChanged(OperationalState::Attributes::PhaseList::Id);
    UpdateCountdownTimeFromClusterLogic();
}
```
The setters do check: `SetCurrentPhase` uses `IsSupportedPhase` (`:60-72`) and `SetOperationalState` uses `IsSupportedOperationalState` (`:74-78`). `PhaseList` reads null when the delegate has no phase at index 0 (`:221-227`). `CurrentPhase` returns the stored `mCurrentPhase` (`:245-246`).
Spec: "When this attribute's value is null, the CurrentPhase attribute SHALL also be set to null." (PhaseList, 1.14.5.1). "This attribute SHALL be null if the PhaseList attribute is null or if the PhaseList attribute is an empty list." and "this attribute SHALL have a maximum value that is "length(PhaseList) - 1"" (CurrentPhase, 1.14.5.2). "This SHALL be populated with a valid OperationalStateID from the set of values in the OperationalStateList Attribute." (OperationalState, 1.14.5.5). All Matter 1.6.1 Application Clusters.
Reachable: no observable effect in the example apps today. No example app calls `ReportPhaseListChange` or `ReportOperationalStateListChange`. Only delegate header comments mention them: all-clusters-app (3 headers), chef (2), microwave-oven-app and rvc-app. A product whose phases or states change at runtime (for example a new program with fewer phases) reaches this code.

Question on the fix: for a null or empty `PhaseList`, the spec gives the value (null), so `ReportPhaseListChange` can set `CurrentPhase` to null. For a `CurrentPhase` that is out of range, or an `OperationalState` that is no longer in the list, the spec gives no replacement value. Should the cluster set `CurrentPhase` to null, or log an error and leave the choice to the application?

**`HandleSkipAreaCmd` returns `InvalidSkippedArea` with `""` as StatusText.**
Why: a controller that shows StatusText to the user shows an empty error message for this status. Any controller that sends SkipArea with an area ID that is not in SupportedAreas gets this response.
Code (`src/app/clusters/service-area-server/ServiceAreaCluster.cpp:415-419` at master `660c0227dd4`):
```cpp
if (!mStorageDelegate.IsSupportedArea(req.skippedArea))
{
    ChipLogError(Zcl, "SkippedArea (%" PRIu32 ") is not in the SupportedAreas attribute.", req.skippedArea);
    return exitResponse(SkipAreaStatus::kInvalidSkippedArea, ""_span);
}
```
Spec (1.6.1 1.17.7.4.1): "If the Status field is not set to Success or InvalidAreaList, the StatusText field SHALL include a vendor defined error description which can be used to explain the error to the user."

The empty StatusText for `UnsupportedArea` in `HandleSelectAreasCmd` (:311) is correct: 1.17.7.2.1 excludes UnsupportedArea from the same rule. Only the SkipArea case needs a text.

Related: #74902, #74904 (other Service Area server items, separate causes).

**`ignoredIndex` is set to 0 and never incremented, so every new area is compared with `SelectedAreas[0]`.**
Line numbers are in `src/app/clusters/service-area-server/ServiceAreaCluster.cpp` at master `660c0227dd4`: :294 (set), :317 (compare).

Why, re-sending the current list: SelectedAreas is `[1, 2]` and the robot is Running. A controller sends `SelectAreas([1, 2])`. Area 2 is compared with area 1, so the lists count as different. The request goes to `IsSetSelectedAreasAllowed`, which rvc-app and all-devices-app refuse while running. The controller gets `InvalidInMode` ("cannot set the Selected Areas while the device is running") instead of Success.

Why, duplicates: SelectedAreas is `[1, 2]`. A controller sends `SelectAreas([1, 1])`. The length check (:289) uses the count with duplicates, 2 = 2. The first 1 matches index 0. The second 1 is skipped as a duplicate (:302) before the compare. The command returns Success and SelectedAreas stays `[1, 2]`, so the robot also cleans area 2.

Spec (1.6.1 1.17.7.1.2): "If the NewAreas field is the same as the value of the SelectedAreas attribute the SelectAreasResponse command SHALL have the Status field set to Success". 1.17.7.1.1: "If the NewAreas field contains any duplicated entries, the device SHALL operate on only the first instance of an entry in the list."

Possible fix: increment `ignoredIndex` after each compared entry, and decide equality after the loop using the count without duplicates (`numberOfSelectedAreas`), not `numberOfAreas`. Incrementing the index alone does not fix the duplicate case.

TC_SEAR_1_3 step 13 re-sends the same list only in a state where selection is allowed, so it gets Success through the normal path and does not see this.

Related: #74902, #74903 (other Service Area server items, separate causes). CHIP-Specifications/connectedhomeip-spec#13700 clarifies the spec wording for SelectAreas with repeated entries.

